Short answer up front
- Most likely you should take the 2023 standard deduction for head of household ($20,800) unless you have substantial additional itemizable amounts (large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large charitable gifts, etc.). Based on the items you listed, itemizing probably won’t beat the standard deduction.
- Your $11,200 graphic-design income is self-employment income → report on Schedule C. You can take a home-office deduction for that business (either simplified or regular method). Self-employment tax applies (Schedule SE) and half of that SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction.
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction but is limited (generally $2,500 max) and phases out with MAGI. Given your income, expect a partial phaseout (so less than the full $2,500).
- Your $720 gift to Habitat is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for your W-2 job) are generally not deductible for 2018–2025. If the $950 is business expense for the graphic‑design work, it is deductible on Schedule C.

Details and calculations (estimates)
1) Income to report
- W-2 wages: $64,800 → on Form 1040 (W-2).
- Self‑employment (graphic design): $11,200 → Schedule C (report gross receipts and business expenses).
- Interest: $1,450 (Discover) + $320 (Fidelity) → 1099‑INT income on Form 1040.

2) Home office (self‑employment)
You have a 140 sq ft exclusive office in an 850 sq ft rental. Two options:

- Simplified method
  - $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction.
  - No Form 8829 required. Enter business use of home deduction on Schedule C (line for business use of home).

- Regular (actual expense) method
  - Business-use percentage = 140 / 850 ≈ 16.47%.
  - Rent: $2,050 × 12 = $24,600. Business share of rent ≈ $24,600 × 16.47% ≈ $4,055.
  - You may also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs etc. (direct expenses to the office are fully deductible; indirect expenses are prorated).
  - You must use Form 8829 (attach to Schedule C) and keep records of all household expenses.
  - Regular method often yields a larger deduction for renters; compare the two. Note: the home‑office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit and can reduce income tax and SE tax.

Important: the home‑office deduction applies only to the self‑employment business (not to W‑2 employment). If the self‑employment business shows a loss because of a large home‑office allocation, there are limits (at‑risk rules, hobby rules, passive loss issues), though for an active sole proprietor small losses generally offset other income. If your business becomes negative after deductions, review tax rules or consult a preparer.

3) Self‑employment tax and related deduction
- Net profit from Schedule C (gross self‑employment income minus business expenses, including home office and any business supplies).
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% on 92.35% of net earnings. You pay the SE tax via Schedule SE.
- You may deduct one‑half of SE tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).

Example rough estimate (to illustrate — use your actual expenses to compute exactly):
- If you use the simplified home office ($700) and have no other business expenses, net self‑employment income ≈ 11,200 − 700 = 10,500.
- SE taxable amount ≈ 10,500 × 0.9235 = 9,697; SE tax ≈ 9,697 × 0.153 ≈ $1,484.
- Half of that (~$742) is an above‑the‑line deduction.

4) Student loan interest ($2,700 paid)
- Maximum deduction is generally $2,500 (and it phases out as your MAGI increases).
- Phaseout for single/head‑of‑household for 2023: begins at $75,000 and ends at $90,000 (married filing jointly has higher thresholds).
- Rough estimate using your numbers:
  - Combined income before adjustments ≈ 64,800 + 11,200 + 1,770 interest = 77,770.
  - Subtracting half of SE tax (≈ $742 from above example) gives MAGI ≈ $77k–$76.9k (rough).
  - That places you inside the $75k–$90k phaseout range, so the $2,500 max is reduced proportionally. Based on the rough MAGI above, expect the deductible portion to be somewhat less than $2,500 (roughly $2,100–$2,200 in my quick estimate). Exact amount requires the precise net self‑employment income and exact half‑SE tax calculation.
- You need Form 1098‑E from the loan servicer to substantiate interest paid.

5) Charitable contribution ($720)
- Deductible on Schedule A only if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot deduct this on the federal return.
- Keep the receipt; if it’s a cash gift, a receipt showing date and amount is sufficient for under $250.

6) Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these are expenses related to your W‑2 job (Metro Consulting) that were unpaid by your employer: nondeductible for 2018–2025 (suspended miscellaneous itemized deduction).
- If they are business expenses for your graphic design clients (supplies, software, travel for that work), include them on Schedule C as business expenses.

7) Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for head of household = $20,800.
- Your only listed potential Schedule A items: $720 charitable + whatever state income tax/property tax you paid (SALT cap $10,000). Unless you have substantial mortgage interest, large property tax, or other deductible medical expenses >7.5% of AGI, itemizing is unlikely to exceed $20,800.
- Recommendation: likely take the standard deduction on the federal return.

8) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income — student loan interest deduction is claimed here).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report $11,200 and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — only if you use the regular method for business use of your home.
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize.
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — for Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (you may qualify; small businesses often use Form 8995). QBI can further reduce taxable income; the calculation depends on taxable income and other factors.
- Keep Form 1098‑E for student loan interest, 1099‑INT for interest, any 1099‑NEC if you received one for contract work (or note that even without a 1099‑NEC you must report the income), W‑2 from Metro Consulting.

9) Arizona state return
- File Arizona Form 140 (resident). Arizona has its own rules for deductions; check whether you itemize or take the AZ standard deduction and how state conformity works. If you itemize federally, that often affects state treatment; confirm with AZ DOR or a preparer.

