Thanks — I can walk through this and give you an estimate of how the 2023 return would look, what you can (and can’t) deduct, which method for the home office makes sense, and which forms you’ll likely need. I’ll also note places where I’m making assumptions and where I need a quick clarification.

Quick clarifying question
- The $950 “unreimbursed expenses”: were these expenses related to your freelance graphic‑design work (i.e., business expenses you paid for your clients), or were they unreimbursed employee expenses from Metro Consulting? (Employee unreimbursed expenses are not deductible for 2018–2025; business expenses are deductible on Schedule C.)

What you can deduct (federal, general rules)
- W‑2 wages: report on Form 1040 (no deduction — this is income).  
- Self‑employment (graphic design) income: report gross receipts on Schedule C; ordinary and necessary business expenses for that work are deducted on Schedule C. That includes supplies, software, advertising, mileage, and any of the $950 if it’s business-related.  
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll pay SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment earnings; half of the SE tax is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment to income (reported on Form 1040 / Schedule 1). SE tax is calculated on Schedule SE.  
- Home office: if you are self‑employed you may deduct the business portion of your rent/utilities/etc. using either:
  - simplified method: $5.00 × exclusive business square footage (140 sq ft × $5 = $700), or
  - actual‑expense method: pro rata share of actual home expenses (your exclusive office is 140/850 = 16.47% of the home). With rent of $2,050/mo ($24,600/yr) the rent portion would be about $24,600 × 140/850 ≈ $4,050 (plus pro‑rata utilities/insurance/etc., if you claim actual). If you use the actual method you generally file Form 8829 (or otherwise show the calculation). Important: the home‑office deduction for the business cannot exceed your net business income; any disallowed amount may be carried forward.  
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 “above‑the‑line” deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule 1), but it phases out by MAGI for single/HOH filers for 2023 between $75,000 and $90,000. If your MAGI is below $75k you’d get the full $2,500 (limited by what you actually paid). If MAGI is in the phaseout range you get a reduced amount; above $90k you get none. (I show an estimate below.)  
- Charitable contributions: the $720 gift to Habitat for Humanity is an itemized deduction (keep the receipt).  
- Interest income: Discover + Fidelity interest must be reported; because total interest (~$1,770) is over $1,500 you should file Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends).  
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: not deductible for 2018–2025 (unless you are in a special category), so if the $950 were employee expenses for Metro Consulting they are not deductible on your 2023 federal return.

Itemize vs standard deduction (federal)
- 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household = $20,800.
- Based on the items you listed, the only clear itemized amount is the $720 charitable gift. Unless you have substantial state/local taxes, mortgage interest, property taxes, large medical expenses, or other itemizable items you didn’t list, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction. So the default recommendation: take the standard deduction.

Home office: which method likely better for you
I’ll show two rough scenarios so you can compare simplified vs actual home‑office treatment. These are estimates; I’ve assumed the $950 is a business expense in both scenarios — if not, the numbers change (ask above).

Inputs:
- W‑2 wages = $64,800
- Self‑employment gross = $11,200
- Interest income = $1,770 (1,450 + 320)
- Rent = $2,050/mo = $24,600/yr
- Home office = 140 sq ft / 850 sq ft = 16.47%
- Student loan interest paid = $2,700

Scenario A — simplified home office ($5/sq ft)
- Home office deduction = $700
- Other business expenses = $950 (assumed business)
- Net Schedule C profit ≈ $11,200 − $700 − $950 = $9,550
- Approx SE tax (estimate) ≈ 15.3% of (0.9235 × 9,550) ≈ $1,350; half SE deduction ≈ $675
- MAGI before student‑loan deduction ≈ 64,800 + 1,770 + 9,550 − 675 ≈ $75,445 → in the phaseout range for student loan interest
- Student loan interest deduction roughly ≈ $2,426 (partial; formula based on MAGI; limited to $2,500 paid)
- Taxable income after standard deduction ≈ (AGI − student loan deduction − standard deduction) ≈ about $52,200 (rough)

Scenario B — actual expenses method (rent portion only shown)
- Home office deduction (rent portion) ≈ $24,600 × 140/850 ≈ $4,052 (plus a share of utilities if you include them)
- Other business expenses = $950
- Net Schedule C profit ≈ $11,200 − 4,052 − 950 = $6,198
- Approx SE tax ≈ 15.3% of (0.9235 × 6,198) ≈ $876; half SE deduction ≈ $438
- MAGI before student‑loan deduction ≈ 64,800 + 1,770 + 6,198 − 438 ≈ $72,330 → below $75k
- Student loan interest deduction = full $2,500 (you paid $2,700, so limited to $2,500)
- Taxable income after standard deduction ≈ about $49,030 (rough)

Conclusion on home office: based on the numbers above, the actual‑expense method (claiming the pro rata share of rent/utilities) gives a significantly larger deduction here (because rent is high relative to the flat $5/sq‑ft). Using the actual method also reduces your MAGI enough to allow the full $2,500 student loan interest deduction. However:
- Actual method requires good records and you’ll typically need Form 8829 (or the Schedule C method for home office) to support the calculation.
- The home office deduction cannot exceed net business income — here it does not (net remains positive).
- If the $950 is not a business expense, your net business income will be smaller and you must re‑check that the full actual home office amount is allowed (it is limited to net business income).

Other tax items you should expect
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe SE tax (~$800–$1,600 based on scenario) in addition to income tax. Half is deductible above the line. Consider adjusting withholding or making quarterly estimated payments (Form 1040‑ES) if you didn’t have sufficient withholding.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you will likely be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction on your qualified self‑employment income (reported on Form 8995, generally) because your taxable income is well under the 2023 thresholds. That could further reduce taxable income; it’s computed on Form 8995 and depends on the final Schedule C net profit and taxable income.
- State (Arizona) return: AZ will have its own form(s) — generally Arizona Form 140 (or 140PY for part‑year). Arizona may have different treatment of deductions and adjustments; check state rules or consult a preparer.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (including the student loan interest adjustment and certain other items)
- Schedule B — if interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 (your total interest ~ $1,770)
- Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (report freelance/graphic design income and business expenses)
- Schedule SE — Self‑Employment Tax (calculates SE tax and half‑SE adjustment)
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the actual‑expense method for the home office)
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A if complex) — to claim the QBI deduction (Form 8995 is the simplified form used by most taxpayers under the income threshold)
- Form 1040‑ES — for estimated tax payments (if you need to pay estimated tax)

Arizona (state) forms
- Arizona individual income tax form (for full‑year residents typically Form 140 for 2023). Arizona may require you to add back or treat some adjustments differently — check AZ instructions or a preparer.

Recordkeeping you should keep
- Receipts and records for the home‑office area (floorplan/dimensions, lease/rent, utility bills, photos that show exclusive use), charitable receipt for Habitat for Humanity, student loan interest statement (Form 1098‑E), receipts/invoices for business expenses (the $950 and any other client‑related expenses), and 1099s / 1098s / W‑2.

Next steps / recommendations
1. Tell me whether the $950 was business expenses for your freelance work or unreimbursed employee expenses for Metro Consulting. That changes calculations and recommendations.  
2. If you want, I can:
   - run the numbers again precisely with the correct classification of the $950 and any other business expenses so you can see exact tax differences, OR
   - prepare a checklist of documents to give to a tax preparer (or to use if you file yourself using tax software).  
3. If you haven’t made estimated tax payments this year, consider whether you should increase withholding or make an estimated payment to cover the expected SE tax + income tax to avoid penalties.

Limitations / disclaimer
- This is general tax guidance and estimates based on the information you gave. Exact tax amounts and allowable deductions depend on full details that can affect AGI, credits, and limitations. For final filing and for any complex situations I recommend either tax‑preparation software or a tax professional.

If you confirm whether the $950 is business‑related, I’ll re-run the numbers precisely and list the exact amounts to enter on each form (and show estimated tax due).

