Below I’ll walk through what’s deductible, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office, and what federal (and brief state) forms you’ll need. I’ll also show quick, approximate calculations so you can see how things interact. This is general guidance — for filing and any edge cases, consider confirming with a tax preparer.

Summary of the facts you gave
- Filing status: Head of Household (2023).
- W-2 wages (Metro Consulting): $64,800
- Self‑employment (graphic design, 2 clients): $11,200
- Interest income: Discover $1,450 + Fidelity $320 = $1,770
- Rent: $2,050/month for an 850 sq ft home → annual rent $24,600
- Home office: 140 sq ft exclusively used for business
- Other amounts: student loan interest paid $2,700 (FedLoan, 2018–2019), charitable cash gift $720 (receipt available), $950 of unreimbursed expenses (please confirm whether those relate to your self‑employment work or to W‑2 employment)

What you can and cannot deduct (federal)
- Self‑employment income and business expenses:
  - Your graphic design income ($11,200) is reported on Schedule C (profit/loss from business).
  - Ordinary and necessary business expenses related to that activity are deductible on Schedule C. If the $950 of “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for your graphic design work, include them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.
- Home office:
  - Allowed if you use the 140 sq ft area regularly AND exclusively for your business (you said it’s exclusive, so that requirement appears met).
  - Two methods:
    1. Simplified method: $5 × square feet = 140 × $5 = $700 (straightforward; no Form 8829).
    2. Actual-expense method: allocate a percentage of your actual home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, etc.) equal to office sq ft / total sq ft. Your allocation = 140 / 850 ≈ 16.47%.
       - Rent portion = $24,600 × 16.47% ≈ $4,053 (plus a proportional share of utilities if you pay them).
       - To use actual expenses and claim the detailed allocation, you would complete Form 8829 and then transfer the allowed deduction to Schedule C.
  - Which to choose? In your case the actual method (about $4,053 just from rent) is much larger than the simplified $700 and likely yields a bigger deduction. Note: the home office deduction can’t create an overall business loss that’s deductible against other income in some circumstances — but with your numbers it looks safe (see calculation below).
- Self‑employment tax:
  - You pay Social Security/Medicare on net self‑employment earnings via Schedule SE. You may deduct one‑half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- Student loan interest:
  - You can deduct up to $2,500 of student loan interest as an adjustment to income, subject to MAGI phase‑outs.
  - For 2023 the phaseout for single/head‑of‑household is MAGI $75,000–$90,000. If your MAGI is in that range, the deduction is reduced pro rata. You reported $2,700 paid, but only up to $2,500 is the statutory cap before phaseout.
- Charitable contributions:
  - Your $720 gift to Habitat for Humanity is an itemized deduction (Schedule A) if you itemize. It’s not an adjustment to income.
- Unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses:
  - Not deductible for most taxpayers (suspended through 2025), unless you’re in one of the narrow categories (e.g., certain reservists, statutory employees). If your $950 is actually business expenses for your self-employed graphic design, include on Schedule C.

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household = $20,800.
- Based on items you listed, likely itemizable deductions are only the $720 charitable contribution (and possibly state/local taxes and mortgage interest if you had them — you didn’t list any). $720 << $20,800, so you will almost certainly take the standard deduction.
- Conclusion: Take the standard deduction.

Quick estimated federal numbers (approximate) — to show interaction
- Total gross income = W‑2 $64,800 + self‑employment $11,200 + interest $1,770 = $77,770
- If you use the actual home office allocation and treat $950 as a business expense:
  - Business expenses: home office portion of rent ≈ $4,053 + other business expenses $950 = $5,003
  - Net self‑employment profit = $11,200 − $5,003 = $6,197 (approx.)
  - Self‑employment tax: net × 0.9235 × 15.3% ≈ $6,197 × 0.9235 × 0.153 ≈ $877
  - Deduction for half SE tax ≈ $438 (this reduces your AGI)
  - Student loan interest: you paid $2,700 but the deduction is capped at $2,500 and phased out with MAGI. Estimating MAGI before the student loan deduction (after half SE tax) around $77,770 − $438 = $77,332, which is inside the $75k–$90k phaseout band → partial phaseout. A rough allowed student‑loan deduction would be about $2,100 (an estimate; exact calculation depends on exact MAGI rules).
  - AGI roughly: 77,770 − 438 − ~2,100 ≈ $75,230
  - Subtract standard deduction $20,800 → taxable income ≈ $54,430 (these are estimates; do not rely as final figures for filing)
- Bottom line: the actual home office method yields a larger business deduction than the simplified method; it materially lowers your taxable income and SE tax base.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 — individual income tax return (filing status: Head of Household).
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (e.g., one‑half of self‑employment tax and the student loan interest adjustment go here).
- Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (report your $11,200 and business expenses, including the home office deduction).
- Schedule SE — Self‑Employment Tax (to compute SE tax and the deductible half).
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home if you use the actual-expense method for the home office. (If you use the simplified method, you do NOT file Form 8829; instead you claim the simplified amount on Schedule C.)
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions only if you decide to itemize (unlikely given the standard deduction is much higher).
- Attach any 1099‑NEC (if you received them) details and 1099‑INTs:
  - 1099‑INT from Discover and Fidelity for $1,770 total interest (you should receive these and report the amounts).
  - If your clients issued 1099‑NEC forms for the $11,200, include/report those amounts on Schedule C (you report the same income even if you didn’t get a 1099).
- Keep your Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) from FedLoan for the student loan interest paid. It will show interest paid and helps substantiate the deduction.

Arizona state filing
- You’ll file an Arizona individual return (Form 140 for full‑year residents) and follow Arizona rules. Arizona starts with federal AGI and has its own adjustments/deductions; check state rules for student loan interest and standard vs itemized deductions on the state return.

Recordkeeping & documentation to keep
- 1099‑INTs, any 1099‑NEC(s)
- Form 1098‑E or loan‑servicer statement showing interest paid
- Rent receipts/lease and utility bills showing amounts for home office allocation
- Receipts for the $720 charitable gift
- Records of the $950 business expenses (invoices, receipts)
- A sketch or floor plan showing office square footage and statement describing exclusive business use

Practical recommendations
- Because your rent is substantial, the actual home office method is likely significantly more beneficial than the simplified method. Use Form 8829 and claim the allocated rent/utilities if you have records.
- Confirm whether the $950 is self‑employment business expense — if so, include on Schedule C. If it’s unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses, it’s not deductible.
- Consider contributing to a retirement plan for self‑employment (SEP IRA or Solo 401(k)) before year‑end in future years to reduce AGI and potentially preserve more of the student loan interest deduction and reduce taxes.
- Make sure you (or your tax preparer) compute the precise student loan interest phaseout based on your exact MAGI — my number above is an estimate.
- If you expect to owe self‑employment tax or additional income tax, consider estimated tax payments (for future years) to avoid penalties.

If you’d like, I can:
