3) Home office — how to treat it and which method to pick
You rent, you have a dedicated 140 sq ft space in an 850 sq ft home (exclusive and regular use). That generally qualifies for the home‑office deduction if the space is used regularly and exclusively for your self‑employment business.

Two methods:
- Simplified method: $5.00 × allowable square feet (max 300). For 140 sq ft: 140 × $5 = $700. No Form 8829 needed; enter on Schedule C.
- Regular (actual) method: Allocate actual indirect home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, maintenance) by the business percentage (140/850 ≈ 16.47%). Your annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600. The rent portion allocable to the business ≈ $24,600 × 140/850 ≈ $4,053. You would also allocate a portion of utilities, etc., to increase that amount. To use the actual method you generally file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with your Schedule C.
Important limitations:
- The business‑use‑of‑home deduction cannot create or increase a net loss from the business for the year. IRS Pub 587: the home office deduction is limited to the gross income from the business minus other business deductions (other than the home office). If your business gross income minus other business deductions is larger than the home office allocation, you can take the full home office amount. If not, some home office expense may be carried forward to later years.
Given your numbers: gross self‑employment income $11,200; other business expenses (if the $950 is for the business) ≈ $950; so gross minus other business deductions ≈ $10,250, which is larger than the home office allocation of ≈ $4,053 — so the full $4,053 would be allowed under the actual method.
