Below is a clear, practical summary of what you can (likely) deduct, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office, the required forms, and a short worked estimate using the numbers you gave. This is general tax guidance — not a substitute for personalized advice from a CPA or tax preparer. If you want, I can run a tighter estimate or prepare draft numbers for your return if you confirm a couple of details (see questions at the end).

Summary — what you can deduct (based on your facts)
- W-2 wages (Metro Consulting $64,800): report on Form 1040 (your employer’s W-2).
- Self-employment income ($11,200 from graphic design): must be reported on Schedule C (Form 1040). You may deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses related to that activity on Schedule C.
  - The $950 “unreimbursed expenses”: if these are business expenses for your graphic design clients (supplies, software, travel, etc.), report them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed expenses incurred as an employee of Metro (W-2), they generally are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.
- Interest from Discover ($1,450) and Fidelity ($320): report as interest income on Form 1040 (you should receive 1099‑INTs).
- Student loan interest ($2,700 paid to FedLoan): you may be able to deduct up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income (Form 1040 / Schedule 1), subject to income phaseouts (explained below). You should get Form 1098‑E from FedLoan showing interest paid.
- Charitable donation ($720 to Habitat for Humanity): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt.
- Self-employment tax: you will owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net self‑employment income; you may deduct one-half of SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
- Home office: special rules — explained next.

Home office deduction — how to treat it
You qualify for the home office deduction only for the self‑employment (graphic design) activity, not for your W-2 job. The office must be used regularly and exclusively for business — you said 140 sq ft exclusive, so that sounds like it meets the exclusivity requirement.

Two methods:
1) Simplified method — $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
   - Your deduction = 140 sq ft × $5 = $700.
   - Easy, no Form 8829; report directly on Schedule C.

2) Actual-expense method — allocate actual home expenses between business and personal use.
   - Allocation = business square footage / total home square footage = 140 / 850 ≈ 16.47%.
   - You said you pay $2,050/month in rent → annual rent = $24,600. Business portion of rent = $24,600 × 16.47% ≈ $4,056.
   - You can also allocate a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., using the same percentage. Those additional items would increase the deduction.
   - Use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach results to Schedule C.

Which method to pick?
- Based on rent alone, the actual method’s rent allocation (~$4,056) is much larger than the simplified $700. If you can document and allocate utilities/insurance/repairs, actual is likely better. Use actual only if you can document the underlying expenses (and understand Form 8829 rules).
- Note: the home office deduction cannot exceed the gross income from that business (there are limits if it creates a business loss); excess may be carried forward (see Form 8829 instructions).

Itemize vs standard deduction
- For 2023, the standard deduction for Head of Household = $20,800.
