- Self‑employment tax: you pay SE tax (Schedule SE); one‑half of SE tax is an “above‑the‑line” deduction on Form 1040 (reported on Schedule 1).
- Student‑loan interest: up to $2,500 (above‑the‑line), subject to MAGI phaseout ($75k–$90k for HOH in 2023).
- Charity: $720 is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot deduct it on your federal return.

Home‑office details and comparison (your numbers)
- Your office: 140 sq ft exclusive; home = 850 sq ft → business use % = 140 / 850 = 16.4706% (≈16.47%).
- Rent = $2,050/mo → annual rent = $24,600.

Simplified method
- $5 per sq ft × 140 = $700 home‑office deduction.
- No Form 8829. Simpler, but usually smaller for renters with high rent.

Actual method (just using rent here; if you add utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, they would increase the deduction)
- Rent portion = 24,600 × 16.4706% = $4,052.94 (approx).
- You can also allocate a business share of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. (multiply each by 16.47%). To claim actual you must complete Form 8829 (or follow Schedule C instructions) and the home‑office deduction cannot exceed net profit from that business; any excess may carry forward (Form 8829 guidance applies).

Which method likely better for you
- Based on the rent alone, actual method (≈$4,053) > simplified ($700). So actual will likely be more beneficial — assuming the home office is exclusively for your self‑employment and you want to keep the records to support allocated expenses.

How the home‑office deduction affects other items
- The home‑office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit, which reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax. That reduction can also lower your MAGI for phaseouts (e.g., student loan interest), which can be advantageous.

Student‑loan interest (your $2,700)
- Maximum statutory deduction is $2,500 for 2023.
- Phaseout for Head of Household filers: MAGI $75,000–$90,000. If your MAGI (after allowable above‑the‑line adjustments except the student loan interest itself) is below $75k you can take the full $2,500. If it’s between $75k and $90k the deduction is partially reduced; over $90k it’s disallowed.
- With the actual home‑office allocation and treating the $950 as business expenses, your MAGI likely falls below $75k so you would be able to take the full $2,500 (so the $2,700 you paid would be limited to $2,500). If you instead use the simplified home‑office and/or the $950 are nondeductible employee expenses, you may be in the phaseout range and the student interest deduction could be reduced.

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 — adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction, half of self‑employment tax deduction is listed here).
- Schedule B — interest and ordinary dividends (required because your interest > $1,500).
- Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (report $11,200 less business expenses).
- Schedule SE — Self‑Employment Tax (calculates SE tax on net self‑employment earnings).
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (only if you use the actual method for the home office).
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions only if you decide to itemize (seems unlikely here).
- Keep Forms 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC from clients if any, Forms 1099‑INT from Discover and Fidelity, and Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) from FedLoan.

Arizona (state)
