Short answer up front
- You will almost certainly take the 2023 head-of-household standard deduction ($20,800) instead of itemizing based on the items you listed.
- Your graphic-design work is self-employment income and should be reported on Schedule C. Because you have self-employment income you can take a home‑office deduction (if the space is used exclusively and regularly for the business).
- Interest from Discover + Fidelity (total $1,770) must be reported and, because it’s over $1,500, you must file Schedule B.
- You’ll likely use these federal forms: Form 1040, Schedule 1, Schedule B, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (or the simplified home-office option), and Form 8995 (QBI) if applicable. File Arizona Form 140 for the state return.
- This is general guidance, not tax advice. If you want, I can run rough tax calculations and show estimated tax due.

Details and why

1) Income you must report
- W-2 wages from Metro Consulting: $64,800 (reported on Form 1040).
- Self‑employment (graphic design) income: $11,200 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Interest: $1,450 (Discover) + $320 (Fidelity) = $1,770 — report as interest income on Form 1040; because total interest > $1,500 you must attach Schedule B.

Note: If you received any 1099‑NEC or 1099‑K for the clients, include those amounts even if you don’t have forms. All income must be reported.

2) Business expenses, unreimbursed expenses, and home office
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for someone employed by an employer) are NOT deductible for 2018–2025. If the $950 are unreimbursed employee expenses related to a W‑2 job, they are not deductible.
- If the $950 are ordinary and necessary expenses of your self‑employment (supplies, software, advertising, mileage, etc.), they belong on Schedule C and are deductible against your self‑employment income.
- Home‑office deduction (you said 140 sq ft exclusive; home = 850 sq ft):
  - You are eligible for a home‑office deduction for the graphic‑design self‑employment only if the office is used exclusively and regularly for that business and is your principal place of business.
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 (easy, no Form 8829).
    - Regular (actual) method: allocate a percentage of home expenses. Your allocation = 140/850 = 16.47%. As a renter paying $2,050/month, annual rent = $24,600; deductible portion of rent = 24,600 × 16.47% ≈ $4,054. You may also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., using the same percentage (you didn’t list utilities — include them if you pay them and want more deduction).
  - The home‑office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit which lowers income subject to income tax and self‑employment tax.
  - Use Form 8829 to compute the regular method and transfer the result to Schedule C (or use the simplified method directly on Schedule C).

3) Self‑employment tax, QBI, and related deductions
- Self‑employment tax (Social Security & Medicare) applies to net self‑employment profit. It’s computed on Schedule SE. Roughly 15.3% of 92.35% of your net profit.
- You may deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (this lowers your AGI).
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: up to 20% of qualified business income from your Schedule C (Form 8995 or 8995‑A). With your small business income, you will likely be eligible for a QBI deduction; it is computed after netting business expenses but complicated by taxable income limits. I can estimate it for you if you want.

4) Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) is an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 if you meet the income limits and other rules.
- Phaseout for 2023 (single/head of household): MAGI $75,000–$90,000. If your MAGI falls inside that range the deduction is reduced proportionally; above $90k you cannot take it.
- Because your combined gross income (W‑2 + interest + self‑employment) is about $77,770 before adjustments, you are inside the phaseout range and will get a reduced amount of the $2,500 maximum. The actual deductible amount depends on your final MAGI after allowable adjustments (half SE tax, retirement contributions, etc.). If you want I can estimate the allowed student‑loan interest deduction for both home‑office methods.

5) Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for head of household = $20,800.
- Your listed itemizable items: charitable contributions $720; maybe state income tax (unknown amount), property tax (likely none because you rent), mortgage interest (none). With only $720 in known charitable gifts, your itemized total is almost certainly under $20,800, so standard deduction is the better choice.
- If you have significant additional deductible items (large state tax, mortgage interest, large medical expenses exceeding 7.5% of AGI, or more charitable gifts), it could change the decision — tell me if you have those.

6) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (additional income adjustments — student loan interest adjustment, deductible half SE tax)
- Schedule B (interest income > $1,500)
- Schedule C (profit/loss from business)
- Schedule SE (self‑employment tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular home‑office method
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — QBI deduction (if you claim QBI)
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (probably not)
Also gather your 1099‑INT(s), any 1099‑NEC(s), W‑2, and student loan interest statement (Form 1098‑E if provided), and receipts for charitable contributions.

7) State filing (Arizona)
- Arizona resident: file AZ Form 140 (or appropriate AZ return form). Arizona generally starts with federal AGI as the base, but there are state-specific adjustments; keep good records. Check AZ instructions or ask if you want state specifics.

8) Records to keep
- Receipts for the home‑office (floor plan, rent receipts, utility bills), receipts for the $950 business expenses (or documentation showing they are self‑employment expenses), bank/credit card receipts for charitable gift ($720), Form 1098‑E or loan statements for student loan interest, all 1099s and W‑2s, and records of hours/dates you used the home office (to substantiate exclusive/regular use).

9) Next steps and offers
- If you want, I can:
  - Estimate your federal tax (income tax + self‑employment tax) under both the simplified and regular home‑office methods and show the difference.
  - Compute an estimate of the student‑loan interest deduction after phaseout.
  - Prepare a checklist of documents to gather before you (or a preparer) file.
- If you want exact calculations or planning (e.g., estimated tax payments for 2024, whether to make estimated payments for remaining 2023 liability), consider consulting a CPA or tax preparer who can review complete records and give binding advice.

Would you like me to run the rough tax math now (estimate net Schedule C profit, self‑employment tax, student loan interest allowed, QBI, and overall federal tax) using the regular home‑office method (rent allocation) or the simplified method? Which one do you prefer me to assume, and are the $950 expenses business expenses (not unreimbursed employee expenses)?

