Short answer up front
- For federal 2023 taxes you will almost certainly take the standard deduction for Head of Household (2023 HOH standard = $20,800). Your only itemizable federal deduction you listed is the $720 charitable gift, far smaller than the standard deduction.
- The $11,200 of graphic-design income is self‑employment income and must be reported on Schedule C (and Schedule SE for self‑employment tax). That makes you eligible for a home‑office deduction (only for the self‑employment activity).
- Student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (not an itemized deduction) — maximum deduction $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseouts). Interest income must be reported; because your total interest (~$1,770) is over $1,500 you’ll also file Schedule B.
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses (your W‑2 job) are not deductible under current law (TCJA) unless you are in a special category. If the $950 are self‑employment business expenses, they are deductible on Schedule C.

Details and how to treat each item

1) Income to report
- W‑2 from Metro Consulting: $64,800 — report on Form 1040 (you’ll get a W‑2).
- Self‑employment (graphic design) $11,200 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Report gross receipts and list any business expenses there.
- Interest: $1,450 (Discover) + $320 (Fidelity) = $1,770 — report on Form 1040; because total taxable interest > $1,500, file Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends).

2) Home‑office deduction (self‑employment only)
- Qualification: must be used regularly and exclusively for the business and be your principal place of business (or used to meet clients, etc.). If you meet that, you can claim a deduction.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 140 sq ft = 140 × $5 = $700.
  - Regular (actual‑expense) method: allocate a percentage of home expenses to the office. Your percentage = 140 sq ft / 850 sq ft = 16.47% (approx).
    - Rent: $2,050/mo × 12 = $24,600 × 16.47% ≈ $4,053 (that’s just rent; you could also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., to increase the deduction).
    - If you use the actual method you will prepare Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and carry the deduction to Schedule C. If you use the simplified method, you do not use Form 8829.
- Note: the home‑office deduction applies only to the self‑employment business. If these expenses produce a business loss, they reduce net profit and thereby reduce income subject to income tax and self‑employment tax (there are some limitations if the business consistently shows losses).

3) Self‑employment tax and related deduction
- You will owe self‑employment tax on net earnings from self‑employment (Schedule SE). The employee share and employer share combined are ~15.3% (applied to 92.35% of net profit).
- Half of your self‑employment tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (it reduces AGI).

4) Student loan interest
- The deduction is up to $2,500 per year (even if you paid more). It’s reported as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1), not an itemized deduction.
- The deduction phases out at higher incomes (for 2023 the phaseout for single/head‑of‑household roughly starts around $75,000 MAGI and ends around $90,000 — if your MAGI is in that range the deduction is reduced). Because your wages + business income + interest are near that range, whether you get the full $2,500 or a partial amount depends on your exact MAGI after allowable above‑the‑line deductions (half SE tax, etc.). If you want, I can run the numbers with either the simplified or actual home‑office method to estimate whether you get the full $2,500.

5) Charitable contributions
- $720 to Habitat for Humanity: deductible only if you itemize. Since your likely federal itemized deductions are far less than the HOH standard deduction ($20,800), you will almost certainly NOT itemize on federal returns, so this donation will not reduce your federal taxable income (unless you have additional itemizable amounts you haven’t listed).
- Keep the receipt.

6) Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these are expenses incurred as a W‑2 employee (unreimbursed employee business expenses), they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under the TCJA.
- If these are expenses related to your freelance/graphic design business (supplies, software, client‑related travel, etc.), report them on Schedule C as business expenses.

7) State (Arizona) considerations
- Arizona uses Form 140 (resident return) for 2023. Arizona’s rules largely conform to federal, but there are differences — you’ll generally start with federal AGI and make state adjustments. Check Arizona rules for standard deduction vs itemizing on the state return. (If you want, I can look up the Arizona specifics or estimate your state tax.)

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — individual income tax return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (student loan interest, half of SE tax is an adjustment; some items from Schedule SE or other adjustments show here).
- Schedule B — if taxable interest > $1,500 (yours is ~$1,770).
- Schedule C — report self‑employment income and business expenses (including home‑office deduction carried from Form 8829, or the simplified home‑office amount on Schedule C).
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home — only if you use the actual/regular method for the home‑office deduction (not needed for the simplified method).
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — if you want to claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (you may be eligible for a small-business QBI deduction; whether you benefit depends on your taxable income and type of business). For many small sole‑props, Form 8995 is used.

Practical recommendations / next steps
1. Clarify two items for a precise answer:
   - Are the $950 unreimbursed expenses related to your self‑employment (graphic design) or to your W‑2 job?
   - Do you have other deductible expenses (state income tax paid, real estate taxes, mortgage interest, unreimbursed medical expenses, etc.) that might increase itemized deductions?
2. Home office: given your rent level, the actual method (using Form 8829) will likely give you a much larger home‑office deduction (~$4,000 on rent alone) vs $700 with the simplified method — but it requires tracking other home expenses and may only be allowed to the extent of business profit. If you want, I can compute an estimate showing both methods and the effect on AGI, student loan interest deduction, and self‑employment tax.
3. Prepare for self‑employment tax and possible quarterly estimated tax payments for the current year. If your W‑2 withholdings are low, you may owe underpayment penalties for 2023; for future years, consider withholding adjustments or quarterly payments.
4. Keep documentation: receipts for charitable contributions; Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer (student loan interest); records of interest (1099‑INT) from Discover and Fidelity; receipts/invoices for business expenses; a clear floor plan or calculation showing the exclusive business use of the 140 sq ft office.

If you’d like I can:
- Run a rough tax calculation estimate for 2023 using both home‑office methods (I’ll need to know whether the $950 is business‑related and whether you have any other deductible items), or
- Draft a checklist of documents/forms to gather for filing, or
- Prepare suggested entries for Schedule C and Form 8829 based on the numbers you gave.

Which of those would you like me to do next?

