Summary of your tax picture (items you gave)
- Filing status: Head of Household (2023 standard deduction = $20,800).
- W-2 wages (Metro Consulting): $64,800
- Self‑employment (graphic design): $11,200
- Interest: Discover $1,450 + Fidelity $320 = $1,770
- Home: renting, $2,050/month = $24,600/year; home = 850 sq ft; exclusive home‑office = 140 sq ft
- Student loan interest paid: $2,700 (FedLoan)
- Charitable cash gift: $720 (receipt)
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $950 — I’ll need to know whether these were for the freelance/graphic‑design work (business expenses) or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job.

What you can deduct (federal)
- W‑2 income: reported on Form 1040, no deduction for wage income itself.
- Self‑employment income: report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You may deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses related to the graphic‑design work on Schedule C (this is where eligible unreimbursed business expenses belong — if those $950 were business expenses, they go here).
- Home office:
  - You can take a home‑office deduction only for the self‑employment work if the office is used regularly and exclusively and is your principal place of business (sounds like you have exclusive use of 140 sq ft).
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5.00 × 140 sq ft = $700 (no Form 8829 required).
    - Actual expenses: allocate a percentage of your home expenses to the office. Percentage = 140 / 850 ≈ 16.47%. Rent portion = $24,600 × 16.47% ≈ $4,056. You could also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. (you must keep records). If you take actual, you generally file Form 8829 (business use of home) with Schedule C.
  - The home‑office deduction reduces Schedule C net profit (therefore reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax).
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) on your net self‑employment earnings (if net > $400). You can deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Student loan interest ($2,700 paid): the federal student loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” deduction (adjustment to income) up to $2,500, subject to income phaseouts:
  - For single / head of household (2023) the phaseout for the student loan interest deduction begins at MAGI = $75,000 and is fully phased out at $90,000.
  - Your rough MAGI (before the student loan interest adjustment) will include W‑2 wages, interest income, and the net self‑employment income but is reduced by the half of self‑employment tax and other permitted adjustments. Because your combined amounts are near the phaseout threshold, the student loan interest deduction will likely be partially available (or possibly fully available if you reduce self‑employment net with the home office/other business expenses). Rough estimate: using no home‑office reduction, your MAGI looks to be a bit over $75k and the deduction would be reduced somewhat; if you take the actual home‑office rent portion (which materially reduces your self‑employment net), you might fall below $75k and claim the full $2,500 (note the deduction cannot exceed $2,500 even if you paid $2,700).
- Charitable contribution ($720): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). It’s a federal itemized deduction (cash gift with receipt is fine).

What you probably should do about itemizing vs. standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household = $20,800.
- Your known potential itemized deductions (charity $720, plus any state income taxes or property taxes paid) are likely well under $20,800 unless you have significant state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc. So most likely you should take the standard deduction.
- Important: The home‑office deduction is NOT an itemized deduction — it’s a Schedule C business deduction and can be taken regardless of whether you itemize or take the standard deduction. So the home office does not push you toward itemizing.

Other important points
- Interest reporting: your total interest income is $1,770, which exceeds the Schedule B filing threshold ($1,500 for 2023). So you will need Schedule B to report interest (and dividends if any).
- 1099s: If your clients issued 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, include those amounts on Schedule C. Even if you didn’t receive a 1099, you must report income you earned.
- Estimated tax: because of self‑employment income, you may owe self‑employment tax and possibly additional income tax; quarterly estimated tax payments are required if you expect to owe $1,000+ after withholding. If you didn’t pay estimated taxes, you might owe penalties — tax software or a preparer can calculate this.
- Documentation: keep receipts for business expenses, charity receipt, 1098‑E for student loan interest (or lender statement), 1099‑INTs, and records supporting home-office square footage and expenses.

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual tax return)
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — for student loan interest deduction (and other adjustments)
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — because interest > $1,500
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your graphic‑design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual expense method for the home office (not required if you use the simplified method)
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize (probably not necessary in your case)
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (you may qualify for a 20% QBI deduction on qualified business income from your Schedule C; this is computed using Form 8995 or 8995‑A depending on complexity)
- State return (Arizona) — state filing requirements and available deductions may differ; check AZ instructions or with software/CPA.

Recommended next steps for you
1. Decide home‑office method:
   - If you have reliable records of rent + utilities, actual method (Form 8829) will likely give a bigger deduction (rent portion alone ≈ $4,056) versus simplified ($700).
2. Confirm whether the $950 unreimbursed expenses were business expenses for the graphic‑design work. If yes, they’re deductible on Schedule C and will reduce SE tax and possibly your MAGI (helpful for student loan interest phaseout).
3. Gather documents: W‑2, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, 1099‑INT(s), 1098‑E (student loan interest), charity receipt, rent/utility records, documentation for the home office, records for the $950 expenses.
4. Run the numbers (or use tax software / preparer):
   - Compare simplified vs actual home office method.
   - Recalculate MAGI to determine the exact student loan interest deduction.
   - Compute Schedule SE and expected income tax + estimated payment/penalty exposure.
5. Consider consulting a tax professional if you want to optimize QBI deduction, confirm state treatment, or if you have concerns about estimated tax penalties.

If you’d like, I can:
- Do a quick estimated calculation showing how much the home‑office actual vs simplified methods change your taxable income (I’ll need any utility amounts and whether the $950 is business‑related), or
- Draft a checklist of the exact documents to collect before filing.

