Thanks — helpful details. Below I’ll explain what you can (and can’t) deduct, whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, what federal forms you’ll need, and a couple of follow-up questions that will change the result.

Summary of your numbers (2023):
- W-2 wages (Metro Consulting): $64,800
- Self-employment (graphic design): $11,200
- Interest: Discover $1,450 + Fidelity $320 = $1,770
- Claimed deductions/expenses: student loan interest $2,700, Habitat donation $720, unreimbursed expenses $950
- Home: 140 sq ft exclusive home office in an 850 sq ft rented unit; rent $2,050/month → annual rent $24,600

What you must report
- Report W-2 wages on Form 1040.
- Report the $11,200 self-employment income on Schedule C (Form 1040). Even if you don’t get 1099s, you must report the income.
- Report interest income on Schedule B if total interest > $1,500. Your interest is $1,770 → file Schedule B.

Home-office treatment
- You can claim the home-office deduction for the graphic-design business only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for that business and it’s your principal place of business.
- You have two methods:
  1. Simplified: $5 × square footage (max 300 sq ft). For 140 sq ft → $700 deduction.
  2. Actual-cost method (usually larger for renters): business percentage × allowable home expenses. Business % = 140 / 850 = 16.47%. If you use rent only: 24,600 × 16.47% ≈ $4,054. (You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., to increase the deductible amount if you have receipts.)
- If you use the actual method you typically complete Form 8829 (Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C. If you use the simplified method, you just enter the simplified deduction on Schedule C.

Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If the $950 are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W-2 job (Metro Consulting), those are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).
- If the $950 are ordinary and necessary business expenses for your graphic-design self-employment (supplies, software, client travel, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C.

Student loan interest ($2,700)
- The maximum student loan interest deduction for 2023 is $2,500 (adjustment to income on Schedule 1).
- There is a MAGI phase-out for single/head-of-household filers: $75,000–$90,000. Whether your student loan interest is fully deductible depends on your MAGI (AGI before the student-loan-interest adjustment).
- Important: claiming the home-office deduction (and claiming any self-employment business expenses) lowers your AGI/MAGI because it reduces net business income and lowers the half of self-employment tax that’s an adjustment. That can make the student-loan interest deduction fully available.

Illustrative scenarios (estimates)
- Scenario A — you treat the $950 as business expenses and use the actual home-office allocation of rent ($4,054):
  - Net self-employment income ≈ $11,200 − $4,054 − $950 = $6,196.
  - Self-employment tax (approx) ≈ $875; half of that (~$438) is an adjustment to income.
  - AGI before student-loan deduction ≈ $72,329 → below the $75k phase-out threshold → you can claim the full $2,500 student-loan interest deduction (even though you paid $2,700).
  - With AGI reduced, you’ll very likely take the standard deduction (see below).

- Scenario B — you do NOT deduct the home office or the $950 (e.g., $950 was employee unreimbursed):
  - Net self-employment income = $11,200.
  - Self-employment tax (approx) ≈ $1,583; half ≈ $791 deductible.
  - AGI before student-loan deduction ≈ $76,979 → inside the phase-out range → student loan deduction would be partially reduced (estimate allowed ≈ $2,170).
  - You still likely take the standard deduction.

Standard deduction vs. itemize
- 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household = $20,800.
- Your known itemizable deduction is the $720 charitable gift. You may also have state income tax paid (withheld), property tax, mortgage interest, medical expenses, etc., but you didn’t list any of those.
- Given only the $720 charity (and no other known large itemized items), the standard deduction ($20,800) will almost certainly be larger. So you’ll most likely take the standard deduction.

Other potential deductions/considerations
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction on qualified business income from the Schedule C. With your small net business income, you may get a small QBI deduction. This is claimed with Form 8995 (or 8995-A). Tax software or your preparer will compute this.
- Half of your self-employment tax is an “above-the-line” adjustment on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).
- If you owe tax because of the self-employment income (and insufficient withholding), you may need to pay estimated taxes or increase withholding to avoid penalties.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income: student loan interest, half of SE tax)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report graphic-design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax
- Form 8829 — Business Use of Your Home (if you use the actual-expenses method for the home office)
- Schedule B — Interest and Ordinary Dividends (because your interest > $1,500)
- Form 8995 (or Form 8995-A) — for the QBI deduction if applicable
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely based on info given)

Recordkeeping and receipts
- Keep rent receipts, utility bills, and documentation showing the home office square footage and exclusive use. Keep a floor plan or photo and note the business use dates.
- Keep receipts for the $950 expenses if they are business-related.
- Keep your Habitat for Humanity donation receipt (you have it) and any 1099s and statements from Discover/Fidelity.

Next steps / questions for you
1. Are the $950 unreimbursed expenses business-related for your graphic-design work (supplies, software, travel, client costs)? Or were they employee unreimbursed expenses for your W-2 job? That changes whether they’re deductible.
2. Do you want to use the actual home-office method (larger deduction usually) or the simplified method ($5/sq ft = $700)?
3. Do you have any other deductions (state income tax paid, mortgage interest, property taxes, big medical expenses) that might push you to itemize?
4. Did your clients issue any 1099-NEC/1099-K, or did you receive no 1099s?

Quick recommendations
- If the $950 are business expenses and you meet the home-office requirements, use the actual home-office method (Form 8829) and deduct the business expenses on Schedule C — that will reduce your AGI, probably let you claim the full $2,500 student-loan interest deduction, and reduce SE tax.
- Take the standard deduction (unless you have other substantial itemizable items).
- File Schedule C and Schedule SE and attach Form 8829 (if using actual method); file Schedule B for interest.
- Consider estimated tax payments or additional withholding for the self-employment tax/income-tax shortfall.
- If you want, I can: prepare a more precise worksheet with exact numbers (tax liability estimate, SE tax, AGI, taxable income) — tell me whether the $950 are business expenses and whether you want the actual home-office method.

Disclaimer: I can’t provide professional tax-filer certification. For complicated situations or to confirm state tax treatment (Arizona), please consult a CPA or tax preparer or use tax-preparation software.

Would you like me to run the detailed worksheet/estimate using the actual home-office method assuming the $950 are business expenses?

