Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether to itemize, how to handle the home office, and what IRS forms you’ll likely need. I’ll also show rough, transparent math so you can see the effect. I’m not your accountant, so consider this guidance and I can refine numbers if you give a couple clarifications (below).

Summary / quick answer
- Likely best to take the 2023 Head-of-Household standard deduction ($20,800). With the information you gave, your itemized deductions (charitable $720, etc.) are almost certainly less than the standard deduction.
- You should report the $11,200 graphic-design income on Schedule C and pay self-employment tax (Schedule SE). That income is taxable even if you didn’t get a 1099.
- Home-office deduction: you can take either the simplified method ($5 × 140 sq ft = $700) or the actual method (a pro rated portion of rent + utilities). Because your rent is high relative to the size of the office, the actual method (about $4,050 from rent alone) will almost certainly yield a larger deduction — use Form 8829 if you choose actual.
- Student loan interest: you reported $2,700 paid but the maximum deduction is $2,500 and there is a MAGI phaseout for single/HOH filers between $75,000 and $90,000. Based on your reported income, your student-loan deduction will be partially phased out — roughly on the order of ~$2,000 (I can calculate this more precisely after we firm up the Schedule C net).
- Interest income: because taxable interest totals > $1,500, you’ll need Schedule B.
- Forms you’ll likely need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule B, Form 8829 (if actual home-office method), and Form 8995 (QBI deduction). If you itemize (unlikely) add Schedule A.

Detailed walkthrough and approximate calculations

1) Income to report
- W-2 wages: $64,800
- Self-employment (graphic design): $11,200 — report on Schedule C
- Taxable interest (Discover + Fidelity): $1,450 + $320 = $1,770 — report on Schedule B (required because total > $1,500)

Total gross income (before adjustments) ≈ $77,770.

2) Self-employment and home-office effects
- You must file Schedule C for the $11,200. Any ordinary and necessary business expenses for your graphic-design work reduce your Schedule C net income (and thereby reduce income tax and self-employment tax).
- Home-office: 140 sq ft exclusive business area / 850 sq ft total = 16.4706% business-use share.
  - Rent: $2,050/month × 12 = $24,600/year. Business share = 24,600 × 0.164706 ≈ $4,050.
  - Simplified method: 140 sq ft × $5 = $700.

So: actual-method deduction using rent-only allocation gives about $4,050 (plus a pro rata share of utilities/insurance if you pay them and they’re not already included). That is considerably larger than the simplified $700, so the actual method is likely better (use Form 8829 to compute).

- If you have $950 in “unreimbursed expenses” that are business expenses for the graphic design (supplies, software, etc.) — include them on Schedule C. If those $950 are unreimbursed employee expenses (work for an employer where you were an employee) they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).

3) Self-employment tax and the “half-SE tax” adjustment
- Schedule SE computes self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare, effectively 15.3% on ~92.35% of net SE income). Half of that SE tax is an above-the-line deduction (Schedule 1) and reduces AGI.
- Example rough calculation (using actual home-office and assuming no other business expenses except possibly the $950 if it’s business-related):
  - If you use actual home office ($4,050) and have no other business expenses:
    - Schedule C net = 11,200 − 4,050 = 7,150.
    - SE taxable amount = 7,150 × 0.9235 ≈ 6,608 → SE tax ≈ 6,608 × 0.153 ≈ $1,010.
    - Half-SE deduction ≈ $505.
  - If the $950 is a business expense as well, net = 11,200 − 4,050 − 950 = 6,200 → smaller SE tax and slightly larger half-SE deduction.

4) Student loan interest deduction
- Max deduction = $2,500. Phaseout for 2023 for single/HOH: MAGI $75,000–$90,000.
- Your MAGI (roughly wages + net self-employment + interest before the student-loan deduction but after other adjustments such as half-SE tax) is around $77k by the quick calculation above, i.e. inside the phaseout band. So the student loan deduction will be reduced, not eliminated.
- Using a rough MAGI of $77,700, the phaseout fraction ≈ (MAGI − 75,000) / 15,000 ≈ 0.18, so allowable student loan interest ≈ $2,500 × (1 − 0.18) ≈ $2,050. (This is an approximation; exact allowed amount depends on final MAGI after precisely computed Schedule C net and half-SE deduction.)
- You should receive Form 1098-E from FedLoan showing interest paid; use that to claim the allowable amount on Schedule 1.

5) Itemize vs standard
- 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household = $20,800.
- Your only itemized deduction explicitly listed is charitable $720; you didn’t list mortgage interest or significant state/local taxes or property tax. Unless you have substantial state income tax or property tax or mortgage interest, your itemized total will be well below $20,800.
- Conclusion: take the standard deduction.

6) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- As a sole proprietor you may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995) on qualified business net income from the Schedule C (subject to rules). Given your business income level, you should generally qualify and it can lower taxable income further. The exact QBI amount depends on final Schedule C net after SE tax and adjustments.

7) Forms you’ll likely need to file (2023)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — student loan interest above-the-line deduction is claimed here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $11,200 and business expenses including home-office portion
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — because interest > $1,500
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if using the actual home-office method
- Form 8995 (Qualified Business Income Deduction) — if claiming QBI
- Schedule A only if you choose to itemize (unlikely given amounts you provided)

Records to gather / next steps
- W-2 from Metro Consulting
- 1098-E from FedLoan showing student loan interest
- 1099-INT(s) from Discover and Fidelity or statements showing interest amounts
- Any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC you received for the graphic-design work (if none, you still report the income)
- Detailed list/receipts for business expenses (software, supplies, advertising, fees, the $950 — please tell me whether that $950 is business-related or employee unreimbursed)
- Rent receipts/lease + records of utilities/insurance that you want to include for actual home-office method
- Charitable-receipt for $720

Questions I need to finalize precise numbers
1. Are the $950 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for your graphic-design clients (i.e., costs of running your business) or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job? If business, they belong on Schedule C; if employee, they are not deductible for 2018–2025.
2. Do you want to use the simplified home-office method ($700) or the actual method (pro-rata share of rent/utilities)? I recommend the actual method in your case because rent × business percentage gives roughly $4,050.
3. Do you have any other deductible items you didn’t mention (state income tax withheld or paid, mortgage interest, property tax, other charitable contributions, IRA contributions, HSA contributions, etc.)?

