Thanks — good detail. Below I’ll cover what you can deduct, whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, and which federal (and basic state) forms you’ll need. I’ll also show a quick worked estimate so you can see how things interact. If any of the assumptions below are wrong (especially what the $950 “unreimbursed expenses” are for), tell me and I’ll update the numbers.

Summary (quick)
- Likely best to take the 2023 Head‑of‑Household standard deduction ($20,800) rather than itemizing given the items you listed.
- You can deduct up to $2,500 of student‑loan interest (not the full $2,700) if your modified AGI is under the phase‑out; with the numbers you gave you appear to be under the phase‑out and should get the full $2,500.
- Your graphic‑design income is self‑employment income and should be reported on Schedule C. Business expenses (including allowable home‑office share and the $950 expenses if they are business expenses) reduce that income.
- Home office: because you rent, the actual‑expense method (allocating a share of rent, utilities, etc.) will probably give a larger deduction than the simplified method. To claim actual expenses you use Form 8829; the simplified method is $5/sq ft up to 300 sq ft (for 140 sq ft that’s $700).
- You will also owe self‑employment tax on your net self‑employment earnings and must file Schedule SE.
- Because your interest income totals more than $1,500, you’ll need to complete Schedule B for interest.

Assumptions I used
- W‑2 wages: $64,800
- Self‑employment gross (graphic design): $11,200
- Interest: $1,450 (Discover) + $320 (Fidelity) = $1,770 total
- Rent: $2,050/month → $24,600/year for an 850 sq ft rental
- Home office: 140 sq ft used exclusively and regularly for business (you stated “exclusive” so that requirement is met)
- “Unreimbursed expenses” are assumed to be business expenses related to the graphic‑design work. If instead they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025.

How the home office deduction works (two options)
1) Simplified method
- $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft.
- For 140 sq ft: 140 × $5 = $700.
- No Form 8829; you just enter the deduction on Schedule C.

2) Actual‑expense method (generally better here)
- Compute business‑use percentage = business area / total area = 140 / 850 ≈ 16.47%.
- Deduct the business share of indirect home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). As a renter you won’t depreciate the home, but the rent portion is deductible for the business share.
- Example rent share: $24,600 × 16.47% ≈ $4,050 (this is much larger than the $700 simplified amount).
- Use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if using actual expenses. The result flows to Schedule C.

Important limitation: the home‑office deduction cannot create or increase a loss from that business. Practically, you compute Schedule C net profit with business expenses (including the home‑office portion). If that would create a loss that the rules disallow, part may be limited; with the numbers you gave actual method still produces a net profit, so you should be fine.

Self‑employment tax and above‑the‑line adjustments
- Your Schedule C net profit is subject to self‑employment tax. You calculate SE tax on 92.35% of net profit, then pay 15.3% of that amount (Schedule SE). You can deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1), which lowers AGI.

Student‑loan interest
- Maximum deduction is $2,500 (above‑the‑line). There is a MAGI phase‑out for single/head of household: for 2023 the phaseout begins at $75,000 and ends at $90,000. If your MAGI (before taking the student loan interest deduction) is under $75k you get the full $2,500.
- With the numbers you gave, after accounting for business deductions and the half SE tax adjustment, you appear to be under the $75k threshold, so you should be eligible for the full $2,500 deduction (even though you paid $2,700).

Charitable contribution ($720)
- Cash donations are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Since your likely federal itemizable deductions appear to be less than the standard deduction for HOH ($20,800), you would not benefit from itemizing for federal purposes, so this $720 would not reduce your federal taxable income. Keep the receipt in case you itemize later or for state purposes.

Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these are business expenses for the graphic design work, they are deductible on Schedule C.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 employer, they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025.

Which forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual income tax return)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your $11,200 gross receipts, subtract business expenses (including home‑office share or simplified home‑office deduction and the $950 if business).
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — calculates SE tax and the deductible half.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual‑expense method for the home office. If you use the simplified method, you do not use Form 8829.
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — required because your interest > $1,500.
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and half SE tax deduction flow here (then to Form 1040).
- If you choose to itemize on the federal return (unlikely given amounts), Schedule A.
- Attach copies of Form W‑2 and any 1099s you received (1099‑INT for interest, 1099‑NEC if clients issued them).

State (Arizona)
- You must file Arizona state return (Form 140 for residents). Arizona generally follows federal treatment for many items but review state rules (some states treat certain adjustments differently). If you want, I can summarize AZ specifics after confirming you’re an AZ resident for the full year.

Quick worked example (approximate)
- Gross income: W‑2 $64,800 + self‑emp $11,200 + interest $1,770 = $77,770
- Business deductions (actual home office + business expenses): home office rent share ≈ $4,050 + $950 other business expenses = $4,999
- Net self‑employment income ≈ $11,200 − $4,999 = $6,201
- SE tax: 92.35% × $6,201 ≈ $5,726 → SE tax ≈ $876; half deductible ≈ $438
- AGI before student loan deduction ≈ $64,800 + $6,201 + $1,770 − $438 = ≈ $72,333 (below $75k)
- Student loan interest deduction: you can take the full $2,500 (limit)
- Final AGI after the $2,500 deduction ≈ $69,833
- Standard deduction (HOH) = $20,800 → taxable income ≈ $49,033
- Self‑employment tax (≈ $876) will be paid in addition to any income tax owed.

Recommendations / next steps
1. Confirm whether the $950 “unreimbursed expenses” are expenses related to the graphic‑design business (if yes, include them on Schedule C; if they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible).
2. Use the actual home‑office method (Form 8829) — it looks substantially better than the simplified method given your rent.
3. Claim $2,500 student‑loan interest deduction (you paid $2,700, limit is $2,500).
4. Take the Head‑of‑Household standard deduction ($20,800) for federal return (likely the best route).
5. Keep all receipts: rent, utility bills, receipts for the $950 expenses, Habitat receipt (in case you ever itemize), student‑loan interest statement (Form 1098‑E), Form(s) 1099‑INT, W‑2, and any 1099‑NEC.
6. If you expect self‑employment income in 2024, consider making estimated quarterly tax payments to avoid underpayment penalties.

If you’d like, I can:
- Recompute exactly with different assumptions (e.g., if the $950 is employee rather than business expense).
- Draft the contents needed for Schedule C and Form 8829 (I can create a checklist of amounts and supporting docs).
- Estimate federal income tax owed (including income tax + SE tax) based on the taxable income above.

Which would you like me to do next?

