Quick summary (short answer)
- Federal forms you will need: Form 1040, Schedule C (profit/loss from self‑employment), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Schedule 1 (to carry Schedule C and adjustments), Schedule B (because taxable interest > $1,500), and Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) if you claim the QBI 20% deduction. You’ll file Arizona Form 140 (AZ return) as well.
- Standard deduction: As Head of Household for 2023 the standard deduction is $20,800. Based on the items you listed, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction instead of itemizing.
- Income reporting: W‑2 wages ($64,800), self‑employment income ($11,200) reported on Schedule C, and interest ($1,450 + $320 = $1,770) reported on Form 1040 (and Schedule B because total interest > $1,500).
- Student loan interest: You can claim an above‑the‑line deduction for student loan interest up to $2,500, but it phases out between MAGI $75,000–$90,000 for single/HOH filers. With the likely MAGI you may only get a partially reduced amount — see the calculations below.
- Home office: You can claim a home‑office deduction for the 140 sq ft exclusive office. Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 (easy, no depreciation).
  - Actual: allocate a percentage of allowable home expenses (140 / 850 ≈ 16.47%) to the business (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs). That percentage of your rent ($2,050/mo → $24,600/yr) = about $4,052 — which is much larger than the simplified amount and likely more beneficial if you have sufficient business income to absorb it. The home‑office deduction cannot increase or create a net business loss (there are carryover rules for actual method components in certain situations).
- Unreimbursed expenses: If the $950 are business expenses related to your graphic design self‑employment, they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible on your federal return (suspended for 2018–2025).
- Charitable gift $720: deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Because your standard deduction is large ($20,800) and your charitable deduction is small, you’ll almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.

