- Interest from Discover ($1,450) and Fidelity ($320): taxable interest, report on Form 1040 (and 1099‑INT if you received them).
- Student loan interest ($2,700 paid to FedLoan): reported as an adjustment (above‑the‑line) on Schedule 1 of Form 1040, up to $2,500 maximum and subject to MAGI phaseout (explained below).
- Charitable $720: deductible on Schedule A only if you itemize; not deductible if you take the standard deduction.
- Home office (140 sq ft exclusive; home is 850 sq ft; rent $2,050/mo): deductible only for the self‑employment activity (not for W‑2). Two methods: simplified ($5/sq ft) or actual (percentage of rent/utilities/insurance, computed on Form 8829). Pick the method that gives the larger deduction (you can choose per year).

2) Home office — numbers (actual vs simplified) and effect on taxes
- Home office percentage = 140 / 850 = 16.47% (approx).
- Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600.

Simplified method:
- Deduction = $5 × 140 sq ft = $700.

Actual method (rent portion only shown — you can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. which would increase the deduction):
- Rent portion = 24,600 × 16.47% ≈ $4,052 (rounded).

Which is better here?
- Actual method rent portion ($4,052) > simplified $700, so the actual method will likely yield a significantly larger deduction (and you can add a pro rata share of utilities/insurance if you pay those). You’ll need Form 8829 to compute the actual deduction and transfer it to Schedule C.

How that changes your business/net income and SE tax (approximate)
(Assumption: $950 is a business expense for the graphic design work.)

A. If you use the simplified method:
- Net business income = 11,200 − 950 − 700 = 9,550.
- Net earnings subject to SE tax = 9,550 × 0.9235 ≈ 8,826.
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 8,826 × 15.3% ≈ $1,349 (you may round on the return).
- You may deduct half of SE tax as an adjustment (~$675).
