Below I’ll walk through what you can (and can’t) deduct, whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, and which federal forms you’ll need. I’ll also list a few follow-up questions and next steps so you can finalize everything.

Quick tax facts for 2023
- Filing status: Head of Household (you said HOH). 2023 standard deduction for HOH = $20,800 (federal).
- Self-employment: graphic design income is reported as self-employment income and is subject to income tax and self-employment (SE) tax.

Income summary (what you must report)
- W-2 wages from Metro Consulting: $64,800 (report on Form 1040, W-2)
- Self-employment income (graphic design): $11,200 (report on Schedule C)
- Interest: $1,450 (Discover) + $320 (Fidelity) = $1,770 (report on Form 1040; because total interest > $1,500 you must also fill out Schedule B)

What you can deduct (federal)
1. Home office deduction (self-employed only)
   - You meet the basic requirements (exclusive, regular use, principal place of your business) if your graphic-design work is done in that 140 sq ft space in your home. Two methods:
     a) Simplified method: $5.00 × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction.
     b) Actual method: allocate business percentage of home expenses. Business percentage = 140 / 850 = 16.4706% ≈ 16.47%. For rent:
        - Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600
        - Business portion of rent ≈ $24,600 × 16.47% ≈ $4,053
        - If you have other home expenses (utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) those can also be prorated under the actual method. As a renter you generally cannot claim depreciation for the home, but you can allocate rent and certain direct/indirect expenses.
     - Recommendation: Based on rent alone, the actual method gives a substantially larger deduction (~$4,053 vs $700). Use Form 8829 (or the Schedule C instructions) for the actual-method calculation. If you don’t want to track & allocate other home expenses, simplified is easier but likely smaller.

   - Effect: The home office deduction is a business expense on Schedule C, which reduces your net self-employment profit and therefore both income tax and SE tax.

2. Business expenses (graphic design)
   - Any ordinary & necessary expenses for the graphic design business (software subscriptions, supplies, advertising, part of phone/internet if allocable, mileage, etc.) are deductible on Schedule C.
   - You mentioned $950 unreimbursed expenses—if those were incurred for your self-employment (clients) they should be included on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W-2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.

3. Student loan interest
   - Up to $2,500 of student loan interest paid is an above-the-line deduction (reported on Schedule 1). You must have Form 1098-E from FedLoan showing interest paid.
   - The deduction is phased out by MAGI for single / HOH filers for 2023: phaseout range $75,000 to $90,000. If your modified AGI (MAGI) is ≤ $75,000 you can claim up to $2,500; if MAGI ≥ $90,000 none is allowed; between those limits the deduction is partially reduced.
   - Important: MAGI is calculated after certain adjustments (including the half-SE tax adjustment). Because business expenses and the home-office deduction reduce your net self-employment income, that can reduce MAGI and may allow a larger student-loan-interest deduction. If you want, I can run the math with your exact deductions.

4. Charitable contributions
   - Your $720 donation to Habitat for Humanity is an itemized charitable deduction (if you itemize). It cannot be taken as an above-the-line deduction on the federal return unless special provisions apply (they generally don’t for 2023).
   - Because the HOH standard deduction is $20,800, your $720 in charitable contributions alone makes itemizing unlikely beneficial unless you have other significant deductible items (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, large medical expenses exceeding AGI thresholds, etc.). Based on the info you gave, you’ll almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.

5. Half of Self-Employment tax
   - You pay SE tax (Social Security & Medicare) on net self-employment earnings; one-half of SE tax is an above-the-line deduction on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).

What you will likely do: standard deduction vs itemize
- Standard deduction for HOH = $20,800.
- Your itemizable federal deductions you listed: charitable $720. You did not list mortgage interest, property tax, large medical expenses, or significant SALT above $10k. Therefore, it is very likely you will take the standard deduction. The student loan interest deduction is an “above-the-line” adjustment and does not affect the itemize vs standard decision (it reduces AGI either way).

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — reports adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction, half SE tax deduction, etc.).
- Schedule B — if total interest (and ordinary dividends) > $1,500; your interest = $1,770 so complete Schedule B.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your graphic-design income ($11,200) and business expenses (including home office deduction if you use the deduction as a Schedule C expense).
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax; one-half of this is deductible on Schedule 1.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — use this if you elect the actual method for the home office. If you use the simplified method, you generally claim the simplified home office deduction directly on Schedule C (no Form 8829).
- Form 1098-E — student loan interest statement (you’ll get this from FedLoan; you need it to substantiate the student loan interest deduction).
- Any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC you received for self-employment (if any); even if you didn’t receive a 1099, you still report the income.

State tax (Arizona)
- You will file Arizona state return (Form 140 for residents). Arizona largely starts with federal AGI, but there are state-specific adjustments. If you want, I can outline expected differences for Arizona or estimate state tax.

Other important points and estimates
- Self-employment tax: roughly 15.3% on net self-employment earnings (after the 7.65% SE deduction mechanics). The SE tax is calculated precisely on Schedule SE; the half you can deduct reduces AGI.
- Estimated tax: because part of your income is self-employment, you may need to have made estimated tax payments (Form 1040-ES) during the year if withholding wasn’t covering income + SE tax. Check whether you owe underpayment penalties or need to pay an estimated amount when filing.
- Recordkeeping: keep receipts for business expenses, your rent statements (to support the home office allocation), Form 1098-E, and your charitable donation receipt.

Illustrative comparison (home-office and effect on AGI)
- If the $950 is a business expense:
  - Self-employment gross: $11,200
  - Business expenses (example): $950 + home-office actual rent portion ≈ $4,053 → net profit ≈ $6,197
  - Self-employment tax (approx) = net × 0.1413 ≈ $875 → half SE deduction ≈ $438
  - AGI ≈ W-2 64,800 + net SE 6,197 + interest 1,770 − half SE 438 ≈ $72,329 → under $75k threshold for full student loan interest deduction
  - Under this scenario you likely get the full $2,500 student loan interest deduction and the actual home-office method looks best.
- If the $950 is NOT a business expense (i.e., unreimbursed employee expenses for W-2 job), those are nondeductible and your net SE would be higher; that could push MAGI near or into the student loan phaseout zone. I can run the exact math if you confirm the nature of the $950.

Questions for you / next steps
1. Were the $950 unreimbursed expenses incurred for the graphic-design self-employment (business) or for your W-2 job? (If business, include on Schedule C.)
2. Do you want me to estimate your federal tax liability (including SE tax) using either the simplified or actual home-office method? I can run both and show which is better.
3. Do you have the Form 1098-E from FedLoan for student loan interest? Any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC from clients?
4. Any other deductible items you haven’t listed (mortgage interest, state/local taxes paid, large medical expenses, IRA contributions, HSA contributions, etc.)?

If you want, I can:
- Run exact example calculations for your likely federal tax (compare simplified vs actual home-office deduction, show effect on student loan interest phaseout).
- Draft a checklist of the exact documents to gather and a completed list of forms to file.

Would you like me to calculate an estimated federal tax bill and show actual vs simplified home-office outcomes? If so, tell me whether the $950 is a business expense and whether you want the actual-home-office method used.

