Thanks — this is enough to give a clear, practical summary and next steps. I’ll cover (1) what you can likely deduct, (2) whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, (3) how to treat the home office, (4) what forms you’ll use, and (5) a few actions that can improve your tax outcome. I’ll also flag a couple of clarifying questions that change the numbers.

Quick assumptions I’ll use in examples below: you are head of household for 2023, you received W‑2 wages of $64,800 from Metro Consulting, self‑employment (graphic design) gross income $11,200, interest income $1,450 from Discover and $320 from Fidelity. You pay rent $2,050/month (annual rent $24,600). You paid $2,700 in student‑loan interest (FedLoan), gave $720 cash to Habitat (receipt), and have $950 of “unreimbursed expenses” (see clarifying question).

1) What you can deduct (federal)
- Student loan interest:
  - Up to $2,500 of student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (reduces AGI), but it phases out for single/HOH filers with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 in 2023. If your MAGI is below $75k you can take the full $2,500; if above $90k you cannot take any; in between it’s a partial amount. You’ll need your Form 1098‑E (or the loan servicer statement) to substantiate the interest. Note: your reported $2,700 paid is >$2,500, so the maximum federal deduction is $2,500 (if you’re eligible).
- Charitable contribution:
  - The $720 cash donation is deductible only if you itemize (reported on Schedule A). You must keep the receipt.
- Self‑employment business expenses:
  - The graphic design income ($11,200) is reported on Schedule C. Ordinary and necessary business expenses related to that work are deductible on Schedule C (examples: supplies, software, business portions of phone/internet, client costs). If the $950 “unreimbursed expenses” are actually expenses for your self‑employment (not employee unreimbursed expenses), they are deductible on Schedule C. If those $950 are unreimbursed expenses from your W‑2 job (employee expenses), they are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension).
- Self‑employment tax:
  - You pay self‑employment tax on net SE earnings (Schedule SE); half of the SE tax is an adjustment to income (reduces AGI).
- Home office:
  - If you are self‑employed and your home office meets the IRS tests (regular and exclusive use and principal place of business), you may deduct home‑office expenses against your self‑employment income. Two methods:
    - Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 140 sq ft that’s 140 x $5 = $700 deduction.
    - Regular method: pro‑rate actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs allocable to business %) using business % = home office sq ft / total sq ft. For you: 140 / 850 = 16.47%. If you only intend to allocate rent, annual rent $24,600 x 16.47% ≈ $4,053. If you have utilities/insurance/repairs, add the business portion of those as well. The regular method usually gives a larger deduction when you have high rent or utilities; the simplified method is easier and requires no Form 8829.
  - Important: home office deduction only applies to self‑employed income (Schedule C). It’s not allowed for W‑2 employees (with limited exceptions).
- Limitations: business deductions (including home office) reduce your net self‑employment income. Home office deduction cannot create a business loss for some situations—there are ordering rules and suspended losses can apply if your business deductions exceed income; a tax preparer can help with complex situations.

2) Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- 2023 standard deduction (Head of Household): $20,800.
- Compare likely itemized deductions:
  - Charitable cash donation: $720
  - You may have state income tax or property tax (SALT) to add, but SALT is capped at $10,000.
  - Mortgage interest — not applicable if you rent.
  - Medical expenses — deductible only to the extent >7.5% of AGI.
- With just $720 in charitable donations and (apparently) no mortgage interest or large SALT/property taxes shown, it is very likely the standard deduction ($20,800) will be larger than your itemized deductions. So in almost all cases here you’ll take the standard deduction.
- Note: if you have other itemizable items (large state tax, property tax, unreimbursed medical, or big charitable gifts), recheck the comparison.

3) How to treat the home office
- Use Schedule C to report the $11,200 gross self‑employment income and deduct business expenses.
- If the home office meets IRS tests and it’s for your graphic‑design self‑employment:
  - Simplified method: claim $700 on Schedule C as an expense (simple).
  - Regular method: complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to compute the allowed deduction, then transfer to Schedule C. Regular method will allow a larger deduction in your case because of high rent (approx $4,050 of rent allocable to the office if you only allocate rent).
- Choosing the method affects net SE income which affects SE tax and MAGI (and therefore student loan interest phaseouts). You can choose either method for 2023; once chosen for a property there are rules about switching in future years (but simplified method is always available).

4) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — to report student loan interest adjustment and other adjustments
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report graphic design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and the deductible half
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the regular method for home office deduction (not needed for simplified method)
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (likely you won’t)
- Attach/keep: W‑2 from Metro, Form(s) 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC if you received them for the $11,200 clients (or report income even if no 1099), Form(s) 1099‑INT for bank/fidelity interest, Form 1098‑E or servicer statement for student loan interest.
- State: Arizona state return (AZ Form 140 or e‑filing). Arizona may have its own forms and adjustments — check AZ instructions or ask your preparer.

5) Other tax consequences and suggestions
- Self‑employment tax: expect to pay SE tax (~15.3% on net SE earnings). Remember half of this is an above‑the‑line deduction, lowering AGI.
- Estimated taxes: because you have SE income, you may owe quarterly estimated tax for 2024 if you expect similar income. For 2023 you’ll reconcile on your return; if you had little tax withheld from self‑employment income you may owe and possibly be subject to estimated tax penalties.
- Retirement contributions: contributing to a SEP‑IRA or Solo 401(k) reduces your AGI and can help you (a) lower income tax and (b) potentially bring MAGI below the student loan phaseout threshold so you can claim the full student loan interest deduction. If that’s attractive, consider a SEP or Solo 401(k) contribution before filing (or plan for next year).
- Documentation: keep receipts, Form 1098‑E, donation receipts, canceled checks, and documentation supporting the exclusive use / square footage of your home office (floor plan, pictures, dates).

6) Important clarifying questions (these materially affect outcomes)
- Are the $950 “unreimbursed expenses” related to your self‑employment (business expenses) or are they unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job? If they are self‑employment expenses, they’re deductible on Schedule C; if they’re employee unreimbursed expenses, they’re not deductible for 2018–2025.
- Do you want me to run two illustrative calculations (simplified home‑office vs regular method) showing approximate AGI, SE tax, student loan interest deduction eligibility, and final taxable income? I can do that with the assumptions above.

7) Short example illustrating the key tradeoff (approximate)
- If you use the simplified home‑office ($700) and treat $950 as business expenses, your net SE income ≈ $11,200 − $700 − $950 = $9,550. That higher net SE income increases your MAGI and may push you into the student‑loan interest phaseout (partial deduction).
- If you use the regular home‑office allocation of rent only (≈ $4,053) plus $950 business expenses, net SE income ≈ $11,200 − $4,053 − $950 = $6,197. That lower net SE income likely keeps your MAGI under $75k so you can take the full $2,500 student loan interest deduction — potentially a larger overall tax benefit even though the simplified deduction is easier.
- Bottom line: in your case the regular method (allocating rent) looks likely to increase your home‑office deduction enough that it could reduce MAGI and let you take the full $2,500 student loan interest deduction. But exact outcome depends on precise numbers and whether those $950 are business expenses.

