Thanks — I can walk through what’s deductible, whether you’ll likely itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll use. I’ll also show rough numbers so you can see the tax impact and ask one quick clarification needed below.

Quick clarification
- Are the $950 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your graphic‑design work (Schedule C), or are they unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job at Metro Consulting? (If they’re employee/unreimbursed, they are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 under the tax law.)

Summary — what you can (generally) deduct
- W‑2 wages: report on Form 1040 (W‑2 from Metro Consulting).
- Self‑employment income: the $11,200 from graphic design is reported on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (supplies, fees, advertising, business portion of internet/phone, and business mileage if applicable), and the business share of home costs for a home office.
- Interest income: the $1,450 (Discover) + $320 (Fidelity) = $1,770 taxable interest (reported on 1099‑INTs) — report on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest: you paid $2,700. The student loan interest deduction has a statutory maximum of $2,500 for 2023 and is phased out for higher MAGI. If eligible, this is an above‑the‑line adjustment on Form 1040 (Schedule 1). Because you paid $2,700, the cap ($2,500) and the MAGI phaseout will limit the deductible amount.
- Charitable contribution: $720 to Habitat for Humanity is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct this gift.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA). If the $950 are business expenses for your freelance work, they go on Schedule C and are deductible there.

Home office (140 sq ft exclusive in an 850 sq ft home; you rent and pay $2,050/mo)
You have two options if the space is used regularly and exclusively for your graphic‑design business:

1) Simplified method
- $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
- For 140 sq ft: 140 × $5 = $700 deduction (entered directly on Schedule C).
- Pros: easier recordkeeping, no Form 8829 required.
- Cons: often smaller than regular method for renters with high rent/utility costs.

2) Regular method
- Allocate business percentage = 140 / 850 ≈ 16.4706%.
- You can deduct that percentage of rent and allowable utility/insurance costs. Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600; business portion of rent = 24,600 × 0.164706 ≈ $4,053.
- If you have utility bills, renter’s insurance, etc., you can deduct the business portion of those too (and any direct business expenses).
- You must complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to calculate the allowable deduction for the home office when using the regular method.
- Note: If the regular method produces a larger deduction, it will also reduce your net self‑employment profit (which reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax).

Which home‑office method to use?
- Based on rent alone, the regular method (≈ $4,053) yields much more than the simplified $700. So unless recordkeeping is a problem or your net business income is too small (home‑office deduction can’t exceed net profit from that business), the regular method will likely be better for you.

Effect on self‑employment and other deductions
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax; you calculate SE tax on Schedule SE. You can deduct one half of the SE tax as an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 — this reduces AGI and can affect phaseouts (for example, student loan interest).
- Student loan interest deduction is limited to $2,500 max for 2023 and phases out for MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 (single/HOH). Because the SE tax deduction and home‑office deduction lower AGI, using the regular home‑office method could reduce MAGI enough to allow the full $2,500 deduction (or at least more of it) compared with the simplified method.

Itemize vs standard deduction (head of household)
- Standard deduction for head of household in 2023 = $20,800.
- Your known itemizable deduction now: charitable = $720. You’d need other itemizable deductions (state and local taxes, mortgage interest, property taxes, medical >7.5% AGI, etc.) large enough to exceed $20,800 for itemizing to be worthwhile. From the data you gave, it’s unlikely you’ll beat the standard deduction. So, most likely you’ll take the standard deduction unless you have significant state/local taxes paid, mortgage interest, or other itemizable items not listed here.

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual income tax return).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report $11,200 and deductible business expenses.
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — only if you use the regular method for home office.
- Schedule 1 — for adjustments to income (some software combines these lines into Form 1040).
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (unlikely based on the items you listed).
- 1099‑INTs and W‑2: include amounts from interest and wages; keep whatever 1099‑NEC you receive for freelance income.
- Keep Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer (FedLoan) showing student loan interest paid — you need this to support the deduction.

Arizona state
- You’ll file the Arizona individual income tax form (Form 140 for most filers for 2023) — state rules differ; Arizona allows many of the same income items; check AZ adjustments and credits. Consider state tax withholding shown on your W‑2.

Rough estimate to illustrate impact (two scenarios)
- Scenario A — regular home‑office method and assume $950 is a business expense:
  - Home‑office rent portion ≈ $4,053; net Schedule C profit ≈ 11,200 − 4,053 − 950 = $6,197.
  - SE tax on that net ≈ ~$875; half SE deduction ≈ $438.
  - Student loan interest: you paid $2,700; phaseout after accounting for half‑SE deduction likely allows full $2,500 (subject to your exact MAGI).
  - Standard deduction $20,800 likely best; taxable income and taxes will be lower than with simplified method.

- Scenario B — simplified home office + business expenses (or $950 not business):
  - Home‑office deduction = $700; if $950 is NOT business then net profit ≈ 11,200 − 700 = 10,500.
  - Higher net profit raises SE tax and raises MAGI slightly, which could partially phase down the student loan interest deduction (but you will likely still be able to deduct most of it).
  - Still likely better to take the standard deduction.

Practical next steps I recommend
1. Confirm whether the $950 are business expenses (Schedule C) or unreimbursed employee expenses (not deductible).
2. Gather documents: W‑2, all 1099‑INTs, any 1099‑NEC, Form 1098‑E (student loan interest), receipts for charitable donation, rent statements and utility bills, records of business expenses (receipts).
3. Decide home office method: if you have rent + utilities totaling what you said, regular method will very likely be more advantageous — keep rent & utility records and complete Form 8829.
4. Prepare Schedule C and Schedule SE (tax software will compute SE tax & half deduction) and compare tax using both home office methods and itemizing vs standard deduction to see which is best.
5. Consider estimated quarterly tax payments in future years if your freelance income continues (to avoid underpayment penalties).

Caveat
- This is general guidance based on the info you provided. Exact deduction amounts (especially the student loan phaseout and the SE tax/half‑SE deduction interactions) depend on details and precise numbers. For final filing, either use reputable tax software (it will test multiple scenarios) or consult a CPA/tax preparer if you want certainty.

If you want, I can:
- run the quick numeric comparison for you if you confirm the $950 nature and any other deductions (state tax withheld, mortgage interest, property taxes), or
- draft a checklist email you can send to a preparer with the documents to bring.

