Short answer up front
- You will almost certainly take the 2023 Head‑of‑Household standard deduction ($20,800) instead of itemizing (your only confirmed itemized amount is $720 in charitable gifts).  
- Your graphic‑design income is reported on Schedule C; you can deduct the business expenses you listed (if those $950 are for the business) and a home‑office deduction (you gave the info needed to calculate both the simplified and actual methods).  
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax on your net self‑employment income and can deduct one‑half of that as an adjustment to income.  
- You can claim up to $2,500 of student‑loan interest as an above‑the‑line deduction (you reported $2,700 paid, but the limit is $2,500), provided your MAGI is below the phaseout (see below — with the numbers you gave you appear to qualify).  
- Forms you’ll need: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Form 8829 (if you use actual home‑office expenses) or use the simplified method (no 8829), Form 8995 (QBI) if you claim the 20% qualified business income deduction, and Schedule A only if you decide to itemize. Also Arizona state return (AZ Form 140 for a resident).

Detailed explanation and numbers (estimates)
1) Income to report (from what you gave)
- W‑2 wages (Metro Consulting): $64,800 (Form W‑2).  
- Self‑employment (graphic design): $11,200 → report on Schedule C.  
- Interest: $1,450 (Discover) + $320 (Fidelity) → report as interest income (1099‑INTs).  
Estimated total gross income: $77,770.

2) Business expenses and home‑office
- You said “$950 unreimbursed expenses.” If those are expenses of your graphic‑design business (supplies, software, phone, travel, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, those are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 federal returns. Clarify which they are.
- Home office: you say 140 sq ft exclusive, home = 850 sq ft, rent $2,050/month.
  - Simplified method (safe and easy): 140 sq ft × $5 = $700 deduction for 2023.
  - Actual method (likely larger here): office % = 140/850 ≈ 16.47%. Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600. Rent portion = $24,600 × 16.47% ≈ $4,054. (If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, other qualifying home costs, you would prorate those too; the example above uses rent only because that’s what you gave.)
  - To take the actual home‑office deduction as a sole proprietor you complete Form 8829 and attach it to Schedule C. If you choose simplified, you report the simplified amount on Schedule C (no Form 8829).

3) Self‑employment tax, half‑SE deduction, and QBI
- You pay Social Security/Medicare on net self‑employment earnings. Rough example using the actual home‑office number:
  - Self‑employment gross: $11,200  
  - Minus business expenses (assume $950 are business) = 11,200 − 950 = 10,250  
  - Minus home‑office (actual) ≈ 4,053.53 → net Schedule C profit ≈ $6,196.47  
  - Net earnings subject to SE tax = 92.35% × $6,196.47 ≈ $5,726 → SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 5,726 ≈ $876.  
  - Half of SE tax (~$438) is an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: if you are under the taxable‑income thresholds for 199A, you can likely take 20% of QBI. QBI is generally your net Schedule C profit (here ≈ $6,196), so an approximate 20% QBI deduction ≈ $1,239. You’d compute and claim that on Form 8995 (attach to the return). Because your overall taxable income (based on the numbers you gave) is well under the phaseout thresholds, you should be eligible for the full 20% deduction.

4) Student‑loan interest
- Maximum deduction is $2,500 (you paid $2,700, so the deductible maximum is $2,500). It’s an adjustment to income (Schedule 1). There is a MAGI phaseout for 2023 for single/HOH filers of $75,000–$90,000. Based on the income and deductions above, your MAGI should be below $90,000 so you should be able to take the full $2,500. Keep Form 1098‑E from FedLoan as documentation.

5) Charitable contribution
- $720 to Habitat for Humanity — deductible if you itemize. You must have the receipt (you said you do). Because the HOH standard deduction ($20,800) is much larger than $720, you will generally NOT itemize unless you have other itemizable amounts (state/local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, mortgage interest, medical above the floor, etc.) that together exceed $20,800.

6) Which deduction (standard vs itemize)?
- Based on the amounts you gave (charity $720 only), the standard deduction for Head of Household ($20,800 for 2023) will be larger and is almost certainly the better choice. If you have additional itemizable items you didn’t mention (significant AZ state/local tax paid, mortgage interest, or other charitable gifts), re‑check totals; otherwise take the standard deduction.

7) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).  
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income) — for student loan interest deduction and half of self‑employment tax, etc.  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report your $11,200 and business expenses (including the home‑office deduction if using the simplified method you report the simplified amount on Schedule C; for the actual method use Form 8829).  
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.  
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual home‑office method. (Not needed for simplified.)  
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — to compute the QBI (20%) deduction — usually the simple Form 8995 when your taxable income is under the more complex thresholds.  
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (likely not applicable based on the numbers you gave).  
- Arizona state return (AZ Form 140) — as an Arizona resident.

8) Other administrative items / records to keep
- 1099‑INTs from Discover and Fidelity (or bank statements if no 1099s).  
- W‑2 from Metro Consulting.  
- 1099‑NEC or records of payments from the two graphic‑design clients (you must report the income even if no 1099s were issued).  
- Receipts / logs for the $950 expenses (or documentation that they are business expenses).  
- Home‑office documentation: floor plan showing the exclusive use area, calculation of square footage, rent statements, utilities if used in actual method.  
- 1098‑E from FedLoan for student loan interest.  
- Charity receipt from Habitat for Humanity (you have it).

9) Estimated tax / withholding note
- Because you have self‑employment income you may owe additional federal tax and should check whether quarterly estimated payments (Form 1040‑ES) are needed to avoid underpayment penalties. Even with W‑2 withholding, small self‑employment income can create a balance due at filing time.

10) A few important clarifications I need from you (so I can finalize numbers)
