Summary up front
- Likely best to take the 2023 Head‑of‑Household standard deduction ($20,800) unless you have substantial additional itemizable deductions (you gave only $720 charity).
- Your self‑employment work (graphic design) will be reported on Schedule C and is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You can claim a home‑office deduction for the 140 sq ft space (you qualify if it’s used regularly and exclusively for the business).
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (max $2,500 for 2023) but phases out at higher incomes — based on the numbers you gave you’re likely eligible for the full $2,500.
- Required federal forms you will likely use: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (if you use the regular home‑office method) or use the simplified home‑office method (no 8829), and Form 8995 (if you claim the 20% QBI deduction). Also include W‑2 and any 1099s/1099‑INT/1098‑E with your return.

Assumptions I used (please confirm or correct these)
- Your W‑2 wages = $64,800.
- Self‑employment gross receipts = $11,200 (two clients).
- Interest income = $1,450 (Discover) + $320 (Fidelity) = $1,770 (report on 1040).
- Rent $2,050/month → $24,600/year for the whole home (850 sq ft).
- Home office = 140 sq ft, used exclusively and regularly for the business.
- The $950 you listed are business expenses for the graphic design work (not unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job). If the $950 are unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses, they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025.
- You paid $2,700 student loan interest but 2023 deduction limit is $2,500 and there is a MAGI phaseout. With the income below the phaseout start, I treated it as the full $2,500.

Detailed treatment and numbers (approximate)
1) Business (self‑employed) income and home office
- Gross self‑employment income: $11,200
- Other business expenses (assumed): $950
- Home office allocation: 140 / 850 = 16.4706%
  - Annual rent: $2,050 × 12 = $24,600
  - Home office portion of rent (regular method): $24,600 × 0.164706 ≈ $4,053
- Order of deductions on Schedule C: subtract ordinary business expenses (the $950), then the allowable home‑office portion (up to the business profit limit). That gives:
  - Net profit ≈ 11,200 − 950 − 4,053 = $6,197 (this is the Schedule C profit that flows to Form 1040)
- Self‑employment tax (Schedule SE):
  - Net earnings for SE tax = 92.35% × $6,197 ≈ $5,725
  - SE tax ≈ 15.3% × $5,725 ≈ $876
  - You can deduct half the SE tax (≈ $438) as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

If you used the simplified home‑office method instead:
- Deduction = $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 (simplified method). That’s smaller than the $4,053 regular method in your case, so regular method is likely better.

Important limitation: the home‑office deduction (regular method) cannot exceed net profit from that business. If the home office deduction would create a loss for the business, excess business‑use‑of‑home expenses may be suspended and carried forward to future years.

2) Student loan interest
- You paid $2,700 (Form 1098‑E should show this). 2023 limit = $2,500 max deduction.
- Phaseout for single/head of household MAGI for 2023 starts at $75,000 and ends at $90,000. Using the numbers above (gross income including W‑2, interest, and Schedule C profit), after subtracting half SE tax your MAGI appears below $75k, so you should be eligible for the full $2,500. Final eligibility depends on exact MAGI after all adjustments; I can recalc if you want me to use exact withholding/other items.

3) Charitable contribution
- $720 to Habitat for Humanity is a deductible charitable contribution if that organization is a qualified charity (Habitat generally is). This is an itemized deduction on Schedule A.
- Since Head of Household standard deduction for 2023 = $20,800, $720 of charitable gifts by itself will not make itemizing advantageous.

4) Unreimbursed employee expenses
- If the $950 refers to unreimbursed expenses for your W‑2 job: not deductible for 2018–2025 on the federal return. If they are business expenses for your self‑employed graphic design work, they are deductible on Schedule C (as I assumed above).

5) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- You are likely eligible for the QBI 20% deduction on your qualified business income from the Schedule C business. With a Schedule C net of ≈ $6,197, QBI ≈ $6,197 and 20% ≈ $1,239 — this reduces taxable income (reported on Form 8995). Because your expected taxable income is well under the QBI thresholds, you should be able to take the full 20% deduction.

6) Estimated totals (rounded) — example result based on assumptions
