Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer first
- Most likely you will: (A) take the federal standard deduction for Head of Household ($20,800 for 2023) and (B) still claim a home‑office deduction on your Schedule C (that deduction is separate from itemizing vs standard).  
- The $720 charitable gift is only useful if you itemize (which your numbers don’t look like they will beat the standard deduction).  
- The $2,700 student‑loan interest you paid will be limited by the student‑loan interest phase‑out; based on your income it will be partially deductible (approx $2,100–$2,200 — see details below).  
- Your $11,200 graphic‑design income is reported on Schedule C (self‑employment) and you’ll owe self‑employment tax (reported on Schedule SE). The home office reduces your Schedule C net profit (and thus income tax and SE tax).

Detailed breakdown and numbers (estimates)
1) Income summary you gave
- W‑2: $64,800  
- Self‑employment (graphic design): $11,200  
- Interest: $1,450 + $320 = $1,770  
Total gross income = $77,770

2) Self‑employment tax and the Schedule C
- Report the $11,200 on Schedule C. Any ordinary and necessary business expenses reduce that amount. (Important: “unreimbursed expenses” paid as an employee are NOT deductible for 2018–2025. If your $950 are business expenses related to the graphic design work, they are deductible on Schedule C — see question below.)  
- You must pay self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment earnings. You calculate SE tax on Schedule SE; half of SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (reduces AGI).

3) Home office treatment (two options)
- You said the office is 140 sq ft exclusive in an 850 sq ft home → business use percentage = 140/850 ≈ 16.47%. Rent $2,050/mo → annual rent $24,600.
- Simplified method: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction (no Form 8829). Easy, less paperwork.
- Regular (actual) method: allocate the business percentage to allowable home costs (rent, utilities, insurance, repairs). With only rent provided, rent portion = $24,600 × 16.47% ≈ $4,052 of deductible home‑office expense. If you have utility/insurance/repairs, add your share of those too; use Form 8829 to compute. That is substantially larger than the simplified $700 in your case.
- The home‑office deduction (either method) is taken on Schedule C. It reduces your Schedule C net profit and thus reduces both income tax and SE tax. It cannot exceed net profit from that business (excess carries forward).

4) Student‑loan interest ($2,700 paid)
- The 2023 maximum deduction is $2,500, but that phases out for single/HOH filers with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000. Your estimated MAGI will fall in that phase‑out band, so you will get a partial deduction.  
- Rough estimate (using the regular home‑office allocation to rent and assuming no other business expenses): deductible student interest ≈ $2,100–$2,200 (software or your preparer will compute the exact amount using the IRS MAGI rules). If you instead use the simplified home‑office ($700), the MAGI changes slightly and the allowable student interest will be similar but may differ by a few hundred dollars.

5) Charitable contribution ($720)
- Deductible only if itemizing (Schedule A). Since the standard deduction for HOH in 2023 is $20,800, and your only itemizable item you mentioned is $720 (plus maybe state/local taxes or mortgage interest if you have them), you will almost certainly prefer the standard deduction. There is no longer a special above‑the‑line cash charitable deduction for individuals in 2023.

6) The $950 “unreimbursed expenses”
- Important clarification needed: are these expenses associated with your self‑employment work (supplies, software, contractor costs, advertising, etc.)? If so, they are deductible on Schedule C as business expenses and reduce net self‑employment income. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job, they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025.

7) Recommendation on itemizing vs standard
- Based on the items you listed, take the standard deduction ($20,800 for HOH) for federal tax. Claim your home office (regular method looks better because the rent allocation alone is about $4,052 vs $700 simplified) on Schedule C to reduce self‑employment income. If you have additional itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, big medical expenses) that push total itemized deductions over $20,800, then consider itemizing.

8) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)  
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments to income) — student loan interest deduction is reported as an adjustment to income here (and flows to Form 1040).  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $11,200 and business expenses. The home‑office deduction (either method) is reported here; with the regular method you’ll use Form 8829.  
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to calculate SE tax and the deductible half of SE tax.  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the regular method for the home office. Do not file 8829 if you use the simplified method.  
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize instead of claiming the standard deduction.  
- Keep these information statements: W‑2 from Metro Consulting; any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC you received for the $11,200 (if issued), 1099‑INT(s) from Discover and Fidelity for the $1,770 interest, Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) from FedLoan, donation receipt for Habitat for Humanity.

9) State (Arizona)
- File Arizona return (AZ Form 140 or AZ 140PY etc. depending on residency). Arizona generally starts from federal AGI, with state adjustments. Rules differ; check Arizona DOR guidance or your preparer for state‑specific treatment.

10) Other practical points and recordkeeping
- Keep documentation for your home office (floorplan, square footage calculations, photos showing exclusive business use, records of rent, utilities, insurance, repairs). The IRS looks for exclusive and regular business use and principal place of business.  
- Keep receipts and records for the $720 charitable gift and any business expenses (the $950, if business‑related). Keep Form 1098‑E for student loan interest.  
- Because you have self‑employment income, consider whether you need to make quarterly estimated tax payments for 2024 (to avoid penalties) if the self‑employment income is ongoing.  
- If you want to maximize tax savings with minimal hassle, use the regular home‑office method (it looks better numerically here) but be sure you can substantiate actual expenses.

Quick numeric example (approximate) — regular home office, assume $950 is NOT a Schedule C expense
- Home‑office regular rent portion ≈ $4,052 -> Schedule C expense.  
- Net business profit ≈ 11,200 − 4,052 = 7,148.  
- SE taxable earnings ≈ 7,148 × 0.9235 ≈ 6,603 → SE tax ≈ $1,010 (Schedule SE). Half SE tax deductible ≈ $505.  
- AGI before student loan deduction ≈ Gross 77,770 − half SE tax 505 = 77,265. This puts you in the student loan phase‑out band; estimated allowable student loan deduction ≈ $2,100–$2,125 (software/your preparer will compute exact).  
- After student loan deduction and standard deduction $20,800 your taxable income will be roughly in the mid $50k range (exact depends on final student loan deduction and any other adjustments).

What I need from you to finalize precise calculations
- Are the $950 unreimbursed expenses business expenses related to the graphic design work? (If yes, they go on Schedule C and reduce your tax.)  
- Do you have mortgage interest, property tax, or other large deductible expenses that might push you to itemize?  
- Do you want me to estimate your federal tax liability (taxes owed/refund) for 2023 with the numbers above and with both home‑office methods?

