Quick summary / bottom line
- File a 2023 Form 1040 (federal). You will also need: Schedule C (self‑employment income), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Schedule B (interest income), and likely Form 8995 (QBI) or 8995‑A if you claim the qualified business income deduction. Use Schedule A only if you decide to itemize (likely you will NOT).
- For the home office you can use either the simplified method ($5 × 140 sq ft = $700) or the actual expense method (your share of rent). Based on the numbers you gave, the actual method (allocating rent) yields a much larger deduction (about $4,050) and will probably be better.
- Your $11,200 freelance income is reported on Schedule C; related business expenses (home office, the $950 unreimbursed expenses if they are business expenses) are deductible on Schedule C.
- Student loan interest deduction is limited to $2,500 maximum and phases out for MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 (single / head of household). Which home‑office method you use can change whether you hit the phaseout.
- Charitable gift of $720 is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). With the head‑of‑household standard deduction for 2023 ($20,800) you will almost certainly take the standard deduction instead of itemizing, unless you have other itemizable deductions you didn’t list.
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax on net self‑employment earnings (reported on Schedule SE), but you can deduct half of that self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income.

Detailed walk‑through with approximate numbers
1) Income to report
- W‑2 wages from Metro Consulting: $64,800 — report on Form 1040.
- Self‑employment (graphic design) gross: $11,200 — report on Schedule C. You may receive 1099‑NEC(s) from clients; include the income even if you didn’t receive 1099s.
- Interest: Discover $1,450 + Fidelity $320 = $1,770 total. Because total interest > $1,500 you will complete Schedule B and report it on Form 1040.

2) Business (Schedule C) and home office
- Home size: 140 sq ft dedicated exclusive business use; home size 850 sq ft. Business-use percentage = 140 / 850 ≈ 16.4706%.
- Rent: $2,050/month = $24,600/year.
- Actual method (allocate a share of rent): Home‑office rent portion = 16.4706% × $24,600 = $4,050. If you only have rent (no mortgage/depreciation), this $4,050 is deductible on Schedule C as part of business expenses. If you have other allocable expenses (utilities, renter’s insurance), allocate the same percentage to increase the deduction.
- Simplified method: 140 sq ft × $5 = $700.
- Compare: actual (rent allocation) gives $4,050 vs simplified $700. Based on rent alone, actual gives a much larger deduction.
- Use actual method when you have documentation and it’s larger; simplified is easier but smaller.

3) Unreimbursed expenses $950
- If these $950 are business expenses for your freelance work (tools, software, supplies, travel for client work, etc.), they belong on Schedule C and reduce your net self‑employment income.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job, they are not deductible on your federal return for tax years 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension), except for a few limited categories.

4) Self‑employment tax and half‑SE tax deduction
- You pay self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) of 15.3% on 92.35% of your net self‑employment earnings (after business expenses). You then get an above‑the‑line deduction for half of that SE tax.
- Example rough calculations (approximate) — two scenarios depending on which home‑office method you choose, assuming the $950 is a Schedule C business expense:

  A) Actual home office ($4,050) + $950 = $5,000 business expenses
   - Net SE income = $11,200 − $5,000 = $6,200
   - SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (0.9235 × $6,200) ≈ $876. Half SE deduction ≈ $438.
   - This lower net SE income helps keep your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) under the $75k phaseout start for student‑loan interest, so you would likely qualify for the full student loan interest deduction up to the $2,500 limit (you paid $2,700 but the deduction cap is $2,500).

  B) Simplified home office ($700) + $950 = $1,650 business expenses
   - Net SE income = $11,200 − $1,650 = $9,550
   - SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (0.9235 × $9,550) ≈ $1,349. Half SE deduction ≈ $674.
   - Your MAGI would be higher and fall into the $75k–$90k phaseout range for the student‑loan interest deduction, so your allowable student loan interest deduction would be reduced.

Bottom line from those examples: actual home‑office method (allocating rent) will likely be more beneficial — larger business deduction, lower MAGI, and likely allow the full $2,500 student loan interest deduction.

5) Student loan interest ($2,700 paid)
- Maximum federal deduction is $2,500 for 2023, subject to phaseout for MAGI $75,000–$90,000 for single/head of household. Based on the numbers you gave, using the actual home office allocation likely keeps you under $75k MAGI so you’ll get the full $2,500. Using the simplified home office could push you into the phaseout and reduce the allowable amount. Student loan interest deduction is an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1 of Form 1040).

6) Charitable contribution $720
- Deductible on Schedule A only if you itemize. With head‑of‑household standard deduction for 2023 at $20,800, a single $720 donation alone is unlikely to justify itemizing unless you have other significant itemizable expenses (state & local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, mortgage interest, large medical expenses over 7.5% of AGI, etc.). So most likely take the standard deduction and you would not deduct the $720 on your federal return.

7) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- You may be eligible for the 20% QBI deduction under Section 199A on your qualified business income from Schedule C. With your taxable income expected well below the overall thresholds for 2023, you should be able to claim 20% of your qualified self‑employment net profit (after the adjustments) — so that’s another potential deduction (Form 8995). That will reduce taxable income (not net self‑employment earnings subject to SE tax).

8) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $11,200 and your business expenses (home office, $950, other supplies).
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax.
- Schedule 1 — for certain adjustments (student loan interest, half of SE tax is reflected here/attached in computations).
- Schedule B — for interest income (total > $1,500).
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — to claim QBI (most likely 8995 if your situation is straightforward and under the thresholds).
- Schedule A only if you choose to itemize (unlikely based on provided items).
- Any 1099‑NEC / 1099‑INT / Form W‑2 you received should be attached/used for reporting.

State (Arizona)
- Arizona has its own rules and conformity differences. Arizona uses federal AGI as a starting point with adjustments. You’ll file Arizona Form 140 (or the short form if eligible). Check the Arizona Department of Revenue guidance or consult a tax preparer for state‑specific treatment (some states allow different election for itemizing; documentation requirements vary).

Practical recommendations & next steps
1. Treat the $11,200 freelance income as Schedule C income. Collect receipts/invoices for the $950 expense and any other business costs.
2. Use the actual home office method (documented allocation of rent and any utilities) unless you prefer simplicity — actual method is clearly larger here ($4,050 vs $700). Keep documentation: floor plan, lease, canceled rent checks, utility bills if allocated, photos showing exclusive use.
3. Assume student loan interest deduction will be capped at $2,500; choosing the actual home office method likely preserves the full deduction. Keep your student loan interest statement from FedLoan.
4. Expect to pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) in addition to income tax; include the half‑SE tax adjustment when calculating AGI.
5. Likely take the head‑of‑household standard deduction ($20,800) rather than itemizing, unless you have significant additional itemizable deductions.
6. Consider Form 8995 for QBI (20% of QBI) to reduce taxable income.
7. Keep all supporting documents (charity receipt for Habitat, student loan interest statement, rent payments, invoices, 1099s, W‑2).
8. If you have little or no withholding for the self‑employment income, check whether you should have made estimated tax payments in 2023 to avoid underpayment penalties — and plan for estimated payments for the current year.

